[[:db/cas 17592186445677 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186445678 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{\\cos }^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{\\cos }^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\cos }^{3}⁡t-4{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186445680 :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{t}+2{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{\\pi }+2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{\\ln }^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{\\ln }^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+{\\ln }^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{t}+2{\\ln }^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{\\pi }+2{\\ln }^{2}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2{\\ln }^{2}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186445827 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡y}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡0}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡y}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡0}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446075 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ since they are the only values that belong to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\,2\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1 \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) & =sin\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+cos\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\sqrt{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The least value is $f\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n![](images/e4/e42ca640ae08d60ea81bdcb7c3c3753f5dc74fb6eea49b5b0f999e3463b5f8f0.png)" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ since they are the only values that belong to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\,2\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1 \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\sqrt{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The least value is $f\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n![](images/e4/e42ca640ae08d60ea81bdcb7c3c3753f5dc74fb6eea49b5b0f999e3463b5f8f0.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186446311 :answer/value "det(A)" "\\det (A)"] [:db/cas 17592186446331 :answer/value "det(A)" "\\det (A)"] [:db/cas 17592186446351 :answer/value "det({A}^{-1})" "\\det ({A}^{-1})"] [:db/cas 17592186446479 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(1)+2 \\\\  & =2+0-4+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =2(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0+8+2 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0-8+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $18$ at the point $(-2,0).$" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+4({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(1)+2 \\\\  & =2+0-4+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =2(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0+8+2 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+2 \\\\  & =8+0-8+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $18$ at the point $(-2,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186446482 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4y,\\,2y-4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y &=0 \\\\ 2y-4x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+1 \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡t+8{sin}^{2}⁡t-32\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1 \\\\  & =9-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (9-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -32\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{8},0),\\,(2,2),\\,(-2,2),\\,(-2,-2),\\,(2,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{8},0)$ | $(2,2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(-2,-2)$ | $(2,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ | $-7$ | $25$ | $-7$ | $25$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,-2).$" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4y,\\,2y-4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y &=0 \\\\ 2y-4x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTh