2,2)$ | $(-2,-2)$ | $(2,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ | $-7$ | $25$ | $-7$ | $25$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,-2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186446486 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x,\\,-2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=0 \\\\ -2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4{sin}^{2}⁡t+3 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(2,0)$ and $(-2,0).$" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x,\\,-2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=0 \\\\ -2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & =4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+3 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(2,0)$ and $(-2,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186446487 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince f has no critical points in D , the list of candidates for the extrema won't contain critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\le 1$, this equation gives the solutions $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval,  $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain candidate points: \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(0,1).$" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince f has no critical points in D , the list of candidates for the extrema won't contain critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\le 1$, this equation gives the solutions $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval,  $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain candidate points: \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186446731 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 5 & -10-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 5 & -10-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-10-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-7) & =0 \\\\ -20-2\\lambda +10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+35 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 5 & -10-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 5 & -10-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-10-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-7) & =0 \\\\ -20-2\\lambda +10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+35 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446732 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(P-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 3-\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-5)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 6}{2} \\\\  & =5,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =5.$" "Let's start by writing the matrix $P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (P-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 3-\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-5)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 6}{2} \\\\  & =5,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186446733 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{13}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{17} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(M-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{13}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{17} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{13}-\\lambda )(\\frac{1}{2}-\\lambda )-0\\cdot \\sqrt{17} & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -\\sqrt{13})(\\lambda -\\frac{1}{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\sqrt{13}$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." "Let's start by writing the matrix $M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{13}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{17} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (M-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{13}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{17} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{13}-\\lambda )(\\frac{1}{2}-\\lambda )-0\\cdot \\sqrt{17} & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -\\sqrt{13})(\\lambda -\\frac{1}{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\sqrt{13}$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal."] [:db/cas 17592186446734 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(C-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -7)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =7$ and $\\lambda =-2.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." "Let's start by writing the matrix $C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (C-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -7)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =7$ and $\\lambda =-2.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal."] [:db/cas 17592186446735 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-0\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-0\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446736 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $U-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nU-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(U-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-5\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 6+2\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -14 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 14}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 9}{2} \\\\  & =-7,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-7$ and $\\lambda =2.$" "Let's start by writing the matrix $U-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nU-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (U-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (U-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-5\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 6+2\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -14 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 14}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{81}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 9}{2} \\\\  & =-7,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-7$ and $\\lambda =2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186446737 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $L-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nL-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -9 & -11-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(L-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(L-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -9 & -11-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-11-\\lambda )-6\\cdot (-9) & =0 \\\\ -33-3\\lambda +11\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +21 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={8}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =64-84 \\\\  & =-20 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $L$ does not have real eigenvalues." "Let's start by writing the matrix $L-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nL-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -9 & -11-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (L-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (L-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -9 & -11-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-11-\\lambda )-6\\cdot (-9) & =0 \\\\ -33-3\\lambda +11\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +21 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={8}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =64-84 \\\\  & =-20 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $L$ does not have real eigenvalues."] [:db/cas 17592186446738 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $U-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nU-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -7-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(U-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ 49+7\\lambda +7\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+14\\lambda +54 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={14}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 54 \\\\  & =196-216 \\\\  & =-20 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $U$ does not have real eigenvalues." "Let's start by writing the matrix $U-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nU-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -7-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (U-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (U-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ 49+7\\lambda +7\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+14\\lambda +54 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={14}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 54 \\\\  & =196-216 \\\\  & =-20 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $U$ does not have real eigenvalues."] [:db/cas 17592186446739 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-8-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ -9 & 8-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-8-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ -9 & 8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-7\\cdot (-9) & =0 \\\\ -64+8\\lambda -8\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+63 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =1.$" "Let's start by writing the matrix $B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-8-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ -9 & 8-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-8-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ -9 & 8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-7\\cdot (-9) & =0 \\\\ -64+8\\lambda -8\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+63 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186446740 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}4-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-9) & =0 \\\\ -12-4\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+18 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1-24 \\\\  & =-23 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $Q$ does not have real eigenvalues." "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}4-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-9) & =0 \\\\ -12-4\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+18 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1-24 \\\\  & =-23 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $Q$ does not have real eigenvalues."] [:db/cas 17592186446741 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ 4-\\lambda -4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ 4-\\lambda -4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446742 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(P-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 25+5\\lambda +5\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{{10}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 24}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm 2}{2} \\\\  & =-6,-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-6$ and $\\lambda =-4.$" "Let's start by writing the matrix $P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (P-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 25+5\\lambda +5\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{{10}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 24}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm 2}{2} \\\\  & =-6,-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-6$ and $\\lambda =-4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186446743 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(C-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-18-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +18) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-18$ and $\\lambda =6.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." "Let's start by writing the matrix $C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (C-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-18-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +18) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-18$ and $\\lambda =6.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal."] [:db/cas 17592186446744 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot 9 & =0 \\\\ -12-4\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -30 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot 9 & =0 \\\\ -12-4\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -30 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446745 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-0\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =7.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )-0\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =7.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal."] [:db/cas 17592186446902 :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}+1)" "\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}+1)"] [:db/cas 17592186446905 :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2})" "\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2})"] [:db/cas 17592186446906 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}({sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}({\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186446908 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}-1)" "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186446909 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186446910 :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186446912 :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186446917 :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186446918 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+5{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+5{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186446922 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi }-1)" "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi }-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186446930 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+1+3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+1+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446931 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{49}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}7\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =7(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{49{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +49{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{49}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}7\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =7(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446932 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={4}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={4}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({4}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({4}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{4}^{2\\theta }+{4}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡4){4}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{4}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {4}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({4}^{\\pi /2}-{4}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={4}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={4}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({4}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({4}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{4}^{2\\theta }+{4}^{2\\theta }{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡4){4}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{4}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {4}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({4}^{\\pi /2}-{4}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446933 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446934 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2(-2)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =4-2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2(-2)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =4-2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186446935 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{(3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9+18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3i}\\sqrt{18+18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={6\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =12(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =12(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{(3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9+18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +9{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3i}\\sqrt{18+18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={6\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{