ned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -6{sec}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z & =-6\\int {sec}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-6(tan⁡z)+C \\\\  & =-6tan⁡z+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z=\\tan ⁡z+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -6{\\sec }^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z & =-6\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-6(\\tan ⁡z)+C \\\\  & =-6\\tan ⁡z+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479305 :answer/value "4cot⁡x+7sec⁡x+C" "4\\cot ⁡x+7\\sec ⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186479306 :answer/value "-3tan⁡x-8csc⁡x+C" "-3\\tan ⁡x-8\\csc ⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186479307 :answer/value "7tan⁡\\theta +C" "7\\tan ⁡\\theta +C"] [:db/cas 17592186479308 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int sec⁡x(4sec⁡x+7tan⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sec ⁡x(4\\sec ⁡x+7\\tan ⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479308 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =sec⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =tan⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int sec⁡x(4sec⁡x+7tan⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{sec}^{2}⁡x+7sec⁡xtan⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 4{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 7sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4tan⁡x+7sec⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sec ⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\tan ⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sec ⁡x(4\\sec ⁡x+7\\tan ⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{\\sec }^{2}⁡x+7\\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 4{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 7\\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\tan ⁡x+7\\sec ⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479309 :answer/value "4cot⁡x-7sec⁡x+C" "4\\cot ⁡x-7\\sec ⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186479310 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int sec⁡x(3sec⁡x+8tan⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sec ⁡x(3\\sec ⁡x+8\\tan ⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479310 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =sec⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =tan⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int sec⁡x(3sec⁡x+8tan⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{sec}^{2}⁡x+8sec⁡xtan⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 8sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+8\\int sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3tan⁡x+8sec⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sec ⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\tan ⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sec ⁡x(3\\sec ⁡x+8\\tan ⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{\\sec }^{2}⁡x+8\\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 8\\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+8\\int \\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\tan ⁡x+8\\sec ⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479311 :answer/value "-6sec⁡z+C" "-6\\sec ⁡z+C"] [:db/cas 17592186479312 :answer/value "6cot⁡z+C" "6\\cot ⁡z+C"] [:db/cas 17592186479313 :answer/value "4tan⁡x+7sec⁡x+C" "4\\tan ⁡x+7\\sec ⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186479314 :answer/value "3cot⁡x-8sec⁡x+C" "3\\cot ⁡x-8\\sec ⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186480008 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡3$ is{{field-1}}." "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-\\ln ⁡3$ is{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186480008 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡3} & ={(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡3} & ={(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480009 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2+2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln ⁡2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡2+2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480011 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186480011 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin