n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +25{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483371 :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=6$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $\\det (A)=6$, find $\\det (B).$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483371 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(6) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =-\\det (A) \\\\  & =-(6) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483373 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483379 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483419 :question/problem "![](images/c7/c700a13da4a3b21940ec6268a89670eee850fc6206285bd3b444959a8b386276.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ shown above." "![](images/c7/c700a13da4a3b21940ec6268a89670eee850fc6206285bd3b444959a8b386276.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={\\cos }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186483419 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){cos}^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)[{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)]}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}[1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{3}\\right)]\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{3}\\right)]}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={\\cos }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({\\cos }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){\\cos }^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)[{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)]}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}[1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{3}\\right)]\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\theta }{3}\\right)]}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483421 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483527 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =2-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2-{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =2-{\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2-{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483528 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+2tan⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186483528 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+2tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)+2({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+2{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\tan ⁡x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\tan ⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\tan ⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)+2({\\sec }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+2{\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483538 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+3tan⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+3\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186483538 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+3tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)+3({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+3{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\tan ⁡x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+3\\tan ⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\tan ⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)+3({\\sec }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+3{\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484193 :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+2det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find $5\\det (A)+2\\det (B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484193 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- For the matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 3-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+2det(B)=5\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3=6.$" "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\det (A)=0.\n$$\n\n- For the matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det (B)=2\\cdot 3-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\det (A)+2\\det (B)=5\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186484198 :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nAlso, given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $M,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({M}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(M)}.\n$$\n\nSince $|C|=\\frac{1}{|{C}^{-1}|}$, we get $|C|=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|B|\\cdot |C| \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det (MN)=\\det (M)\\cdot \\det (N).\n$$\n\nAlso, given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $M,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det ({M}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{\\det (M)}.\n$$\n\nSince $|C|=\\frac{1}{|{C}^{-1}|}$, we get $|C|=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|B|\\cdot |C| \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484199 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\det (A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484199 :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(8-(-3))\\cdot (-5-1) \\\\  & =11\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det (MN)=\\det (M)\\cdot \\det (N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\det \\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\det \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(8-(-3))\\cdot (-5-1) \\\\  & =11\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484208 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{3}{5}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{3}{5}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484234 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(H).$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\det (H).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484234 :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(H)=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(2-(-6)\\cdot 3(2-3) \\\\  & =32\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det (MN)=\\det (M)\\cdot \\det (N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\det (H)=\\det \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\det \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(2-(-6)\\cdot 3(2-3) \\\\  & =32\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484275 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =2‖⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =2‖⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485514 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. If $A=PB{P}^{-1}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$\n\nII. $PQ$ is always orthogonal\n\nIII. $2P+Q$ is **not** always orthogonal" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. If $A=PB{P}^{-1}$ then $|\\det (A)|=|\\det (B)|$\n\nII. $PQ$ is always orthogonal\n\nIII. $2P+Q$ is **not** always orthogonal"] [:db/cas 17592186485514 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(PB{P}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(PB{P}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(P)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({P}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =(PQ)\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. As a counterexample, consider the following sum involving two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing $|\\det (A)|,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\det (A)| & =|\\det (PB{P}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|\\det (PB{P}^{T})| \\\\  & =|\\det (P)|\\cdot |\\det (B)|\\cdot |\\det ({P}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\det (B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|\\det (B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =(PQ)\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. As a counterexample, consider the following sum involving two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186486378 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=-\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{sin}^{3}⁡t,{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ T(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩ \\\\ N(-\\pi ) & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=-\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{\\sin }^{3}⁡t,{\\cos }^{3}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ T(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩ \\\\ N(-\\pi ) & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."] [:db/cas 17592186486378 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that  the position vector of the point where $t=-\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(-\\pi )=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),{cos}^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )⟩=⟨0,-1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(-\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-\\pi )\\times N(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨0,-1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|\\,0-4+3\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that  the position vector of the point where $t=-\\pi $ is\n\n$$\np=r(-\\pi )=⟨{\\sin }^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),{\\cos }^{3}⁡(-\\pi ),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )⟩=⟨0,-1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-\\pi )$ and the principal normal $N(-\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-\\pi )\\times N(-\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,-4⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨0,-1,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}|\\,0-4+3\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486413 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),-(0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),-(0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),(3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+3{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486433 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),2\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)),0-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,1,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t+1)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),2\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)-0,-(-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(t+1)-{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t+1)),0-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,1,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486436 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨({\\sin }^{2}⁡t-(-{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)),-(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486439 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 7-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35+12 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =\\det (A) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 7-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35+12 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486440 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡5t+25{cos}^{2}⁡5t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & -1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & -1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t-0,-({cos}^{2}⁡5t+{sin}^{2}⁡5t),0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{25{\\sin }^{2}⁡5t+25{\\cos }^{2}⁡5t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & -1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡5t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t & -1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t-0,-({\\cos }^{2}⁡5t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡5t),0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t,\\,-1,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486620 :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" "$\\inf (A)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186486626 :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A" "\\inf (A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186486655 :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186486661 :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" "\\inf (A)=2\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186486679 :answer/value "sup(C)=-2\\in C" "\\sup (C)=-2\\in C"] [:db/cas 17592186486690 :answer/value "inf(A)=3\\in A" "\\inf (A)=3\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186486696 :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A" "\\inf (A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186486698 :answer/value "inf(A)=1\\in A" "\\inf (A)=1\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186486707 :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" "$\\inf (A)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186486717 :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186486718 :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" "$\\inf (A)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186486738 :answer/value "inf(A)=3\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=3\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186486799 :answer/value "$sup(C)$ does not exist" "$\\sup (C)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186486818 :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\in A" "\\inf (A)=-1\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186486840 :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\infty " "\\inf (A)=-\\infty "] [:db/cas 17592186486842 :answer/value "sup(C)=-1\\notin C" "\\sup (C)=-1\\notin C"] [:db/cas 17592186486867 :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" "$\\inf (A)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186486873 :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" "\\inf (A)=2\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186486904 :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" "\\inf (A)=2\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186486905 :answer/value "sup(C)=-2\\notin C" "\\sup (C)=-2\\notin C"] [:db/cas 17592186486922 :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=2\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186486929 :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186486931 :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A" "\\inf (A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186486984 :answer/value "inf(A)=0\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=0\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186486996 :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=2\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186487005 :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" "$\\inf (A)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186487019 :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A" "\\inf (A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186487020 :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186487023 :answer/value "inf(A)=1\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=1\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186487033 :answer/value "sup(C)=-1\\in C" "\\sup (C)=-1\\in C"] [:db/cas 17592186487058 :answer/value "inf(A)=0\\in A" "\\inf (A)=0\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186487059 :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=-1\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186487077 :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\in A" "\\inf (A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186487101 :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=2\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186487117 :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186487294 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/35/351e7bb3db6811be2a690fbd3fc2dc99282c4ff676e9116717d85515a298cf80.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $3.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=3-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A.\n$$" "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/35/351e7bb3db6811be2a690fbd3fc2dc99282c4ff676e9116717d85515a298cf80.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $3.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\min (A)={a}_{1}=3-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\n\\inf (A)=\\max (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\inf (A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487298 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,1).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 1$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<1.$\n\nTherefore, $[1,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[1,\\infty )=1.\n$$" "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,1).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 1$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<1.$\n\nTherefore, $[1,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=\\sup (A)=\\min [1,\\infty )=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487328 :question/problem "What is the infimum of the set $B=\\{-0.15,\\,-0.2,\\,0.05,\\,0,\\,9\\}?$\n\n$inf(B)=$\n{{field-1}}" "What is the infimum of the set $B=\\{-0.15,\\,-0.2,\\,0.05,\\,0,\\,9\\}?$\n\n$\\inf (B)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186487328 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a set $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the infimum of $B$ equals its minimum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(B) & =min\\{-0.15,\\,-0.2,\\,0.05,\\,0,\\,9\\}=-0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The infimum of a set $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the infimum of $B$ equals its minimum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\inf (B) & =\\min \\{-0.15,\\,-0.2,\\,0.05,\\,0,\\,9\\}=-0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487329 :question/problem "What is the supremum of the set $A=\\{-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2\\}?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "What is the supremum of the set $A=\\{-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2\\}?$\n\n$\\sup (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186487329 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2\\}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sup (A) & =\\max \\{-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2\\}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487336 :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5-n}:n\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5-n}:n\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$\\sup (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186487336 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-0} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-1} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-3} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-0} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-1} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-3} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sup (A) & =\\max \\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487338 :question/problem "Consider the set $B=(-1,\\infty ).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}." "Consider the set $B=(-1,\\infty ).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $\\inf (B)$ and $\\sup (B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $\\inf (B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $\\sup (B)$ {{field-6}}."] [:db/cas 17592186487338 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-1]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded above, $B$ has no upper bounds, and $sup(B)=+\\infty .$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-1.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=+\\infty .$" "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-1]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\n\\inf (B)=\\max (-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded above, $B$ has no upper bounds, and $\\sup (B)=+\\infty .$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded below},$ we have $\\inf (B)=-1.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded above},$ we have $\\sup (B)=+\\infty .$"] [:db/cas 17592186487344 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/78/7879f99e6dd09e7ada4c1d920b78504df70bf0c511380ea21728a755d6590d23.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $2.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le 2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $2+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>2.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,2]=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $2$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=2\\notin A.\n$$" "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/78/7879f99e6dd09e7ada4c1d920b78504df70bf0c511380ea21728a755d6590d23.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $2.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le 2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $2+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>2.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\n\\inf (A)=\\max (-\\infty ,2]=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $2$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\inf (A)=2\\notin A.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487350 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-2,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-2]=-2.\n$$" "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-2,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=\\inf (A)=\\max (-\\infty ,-2]=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487359 :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{n+2}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $A?$\n\n$inf(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{n+2}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $A?$\n\n$\\inf (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186487359 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a set $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the infimum of $A$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1)+2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)+2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)+2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)+2} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(A) & =min\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}=\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The infimum of a set $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the infimum of $A$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1)+2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)+2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)+2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)+2} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\inf (A) & =\\min \\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}=\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487363 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/72/72fa47f25042b5187a87262b23e0976429909011b1516bc6018e70cd4f881f11.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=2-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A.\n$$" "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/72/72fa47f25042b5187a87262b23e0976429909011b1516bc6018e70cd4f881f11.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\min (A)={a}_{1}=2-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\n\\inf (A)=\\max (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\inf (A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487366 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/42/42f6ea3d1c14b0