 for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $C,$ and\n\n$$\n\\sup (C)=\\min [-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is also the maximum of $C.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $C$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge -1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sup (C)=-1\\in C.$"] [:db/cas 17592186487389 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186487389 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,(x){|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}x\\left(\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{log}_{5}⁡(({e}^{3})^{3})\\,({e}^{3})-{log}_{5}⁡({1}^{3})\\,(1)]-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡({e}^{9})-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}x{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{3}+3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}{\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{3})\\,(x){|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}x\\left(\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{\\log }_{5}⁡(({e}^{3})^{3})\\,({e}^{3})-{\\log }_{5}⁡({1}^{3})\\,(1)]-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3}{\\log }_{5}⁡({e}^{9})-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}x{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{3}+3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487390 :question/problem "What is the supremum of the set $B=\\{-5,\\,4,\\,1,\\,0,\\,-3\\}?$\n\n$sup(B)=$\n{{field-1}}" "What is the supremum of the set $B=\\{-5,\\,4,\\,1,\\,0,\\,-3\\}?$\n\n$\\sup (B)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186487390 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $B$ is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the supremum of $B$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(B) & =max\\{-5,\\,4,\\,1,\\,0,\\,-3\\}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The supremum of a set $B$ is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the supremum of $B$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sup (B) & =\\max \\{-5,\\,4,\\,1,\\,0,\\,-3\\}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487393 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487393 :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡(5x))(2\\sqrt{x})-\\int (2\\sqrt{x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{x}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$" "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={\\log }_{2}⁡(5x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\log }_{2}⁡(5x)}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({\\log }_{2}⁡(5x))(2\\sqrt{x})-\\int (2\\sqrt{x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{x}{\\log }_{2}⁡(5x)-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)-2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${\\log }_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186487396 :question/problem "Consider the set $A=[0,\\infty ).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(A)$ and $sup(A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(A)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(A)$ {{field-6}}." "Consider the set $A=[0,\\infty ).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $\\inf (A)$ and $\\sup (A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-3}}, we have $\\inf (A)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-5}}, we have $\\sup (A)$ {{field-6}}."] [:db/cas 17592186487396 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le 0$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,0]$ contains only lower bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>0$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,0]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,0]=0.\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSince $A$ is unbounded above, $A$ has no upper bounds, and $sup(A)=+\\infty .$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{bounded below},$ we have $inf(A)=0.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{unbounded above},$ we have $sup(A)=+\\infty .$" "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le 0$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,0]$ contains only lower bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>0$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,0]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n\\inf (A)=\\max (-\\infty ,0]=0.\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSince $A$ is unbounded above, $A$ has no upper bounds, and $\\sup (A)=+\\infty .$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{bounded below},$ we have $\\inf (A)=0.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{unbounded above},$ we have $\\sup (A)=+\\infty .$"] [:db/cas 17592186487399 :question/problem "Consider the set $B=(-\\infty ,\\pi \\,].$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}." "Consider the set $B=(-\\infty ,\\pi \\,].$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $\\inf (B)$ and $\\sup (B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $\\inf (B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $\\sup (B)$ {{field-6}}."] [:db/cas 17592186487399 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded below, $B$ has no lower bounds, and $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\pi $ is greater than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $[\\pi ,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\pi $ is **not** an upper bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $[\\pi ,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(B)=min\\,[\\pi ,\\infty )=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we 