to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{2}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** an upper bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(B)=min\\,[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=\\sqrt{2}.$" "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded below, $B$ has no lower bounds, so $\\inf (B)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{2}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** an upper bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\n\\sup (B)=\\min \\,[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $\\inf (B)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $\\sup (B)=\\sqrt{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186488210 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $y$ for any $y>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$" "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $y$ for any $y>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=\\inf (A)=\\max (-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488226 :question/problem "Consider the set $D,$ defined as\n\n$$\nD=\\{n-{n}^{2}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $D?$\n\n$inf(D)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the set $D,$ defined as\n\n$$\nD=\\{n-{n}^{2}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $D?$\n\n$\\inf (D)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186488226 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a set $D$ is the greatest lower bound of $D.$\n\nIf $D$ is a finite set, the infimum of $D$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $D:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2-{2}^{2} & =-2 \\\\ 3-{3}^{2} & =-6 \\\\ 4-{4}^{2} & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(D) & =min\\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The infimum of a set $D$ is the greatest lower bound of $D.$\n\nIf $D$ is a finite set, the infimum of $D$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $D:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2-{2}^{2} & =-2 \\\\ 3-{3}^{2} & =-6 \\\\ 4-{4}^{2} & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\inf (D) & =\\min \\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488237 :question/problem "What is the supremum of the set $C=\\{-4,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,1\\}?$\n\n$sup(C)=$\n{{field-1}}" "What is the supremum of the set $C=\\{-4,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,1\\}?$\n\n$\\sup (C)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186488237 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $C$ is the smallest upper bound of $C.$\n\nIf $C$ is a finite set, the supremum of $C$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(C) & =max\\{-4,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,1\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The supremum of a set $C$ is the smallest upper bound of $C.$\n\nIf $C$ is a finite set, the supremum of $C$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sup (C) & =\\max \\{-4,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,1\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488264 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}\\cdot (n-1),\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/58/5812e3dd22994d613ecc84087418cddfa978e98c1324869f290e1bf2b1317bad.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating, non-convergent sequence. Moreover, $A$ is unbounded above. In other words, for any real number $x,$ we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ such that ${a}_{n}$ is greater than $x.$ Therefore, $A$ has no upper bounds and $sup(A)=+\\infty .$" "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}\\cdot (n-1),\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/58/5812e3dd22994d613ecc84087418cddfa978e98c1324869f290e1bf2b1317bad.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating, non-convergent sequence. Moreover, $A$ is unbounded above. In other words, for any real number $x,$ we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ such that ${a}_{n}$ is greater than $x.$ Therefore, $A$ has no upper bounds and $\\sup (A)=+\\infty .$"] [:db/cas 17592186488268 :question/problem "Consider the set $B=[-1,2).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $inf(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." "Consider the set $B=[-1,2).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $\\inf (B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of 