The set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le -1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\inf (B)=-1\\in B.$"] [:db/cas 17592186488271 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-2,3].$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 3$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<3$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $3,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[3,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[3,\\infty )=3.\n$$" "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-2,3].$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 3$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<3$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $3,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[3,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=\\sup (A)=\\min [3,\\infty )=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186488350 :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{e}^{x}+{4}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{e}^{x}+{4}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{\\ln ⁡4}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/