_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=7-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/ca/cae6f56db55b49b98df6ce7e3ae0777e725af14de9dedb5b61b54039bac41860.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $7.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\ge 7$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x<7$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $7-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}$ is greater than $x$ for any $x<7.$\n\nTherefore, $[7,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[7,\\infty )=7.\n$$\n\nNotice that $7$ is *not* the maximum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no maximum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=7\\notin A.\n$$" "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=7-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/ca/cae6f56db55b49b98df6ce7e3ae0777e725af14de9dedb5b61b54039bac41860.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $7.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\ge 7$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x<7$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $7-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}$ is greater than $x$ for any $x<7.$\n\nTherefore, $[7,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\min [7,\\infty )=7.\n$$\n\nNotice that $7$ is *not* the maximum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no maximum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=7\\notin A.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489208 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 2$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<2$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<2.$\n\nTherefore, $[2,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[2,\\infty )=2.\n$$" "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 2$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<2$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<2.$\n\nTherefore, $[2,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=\\sup (A)=\\min [2,\\infty )=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489235 :question/problem "Consider the set $A=(0,2).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(A).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." "Consider the set $A=(0,2).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $\\sup (A).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}."] [:db/cas 17592186489235 :question/worked-solution "The