$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-5.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-5]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n\\inf (A)=\\max (-\\infty ,-5]=-5.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-5\\notin A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le -5\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\inf (A)=-5\\notin A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186489264 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3+2{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/05/05737c6e8e7b88f1a91a5fd6cc7d5b6a088227e327ef66e937e279c090a47256.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $3.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{1}=3+2{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{21}{5}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A.\n$$" "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3+2{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/05/05737c6e8e7b88f1a91a5fd6cc7d5b6a088227e327ef66e937e279c090a47256.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $3.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\max (A)={a}_{1}=3+2{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\min [\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{21}{5}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489295 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,4].$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 4$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<4$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $4,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[4,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[4,\\infty )=4.\n$$" "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,4].$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 4$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<4$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $4,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[4,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=\\sup (A)=\\min [4,\\infty )=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489392 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={sin}^{3}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1).$" "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={\\sin }^{3}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186489392 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v=1{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1))^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1))\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v=1{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1))^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1))\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6x{\\sin }^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489393 :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" "-4{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489394 :answer/value "12\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" "12\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{\\sec }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489395 :answer/value "6x{sin}^{3}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{3}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" "6x{\\sin }^{3}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,{\\cos }^{3}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186489396 :answer/value "1{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" "1{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\sec }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489399 :answer/value "3x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" "3x{\\sin }^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186489405 :answer/value "2x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" "2x{\\sin }^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186489407 :answer/value "8{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" "8{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489408 :answer/value "8\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" "8\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{\\sec }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489413 :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" "-4{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489414 :answer/value "4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" "4{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489418 :answer/value "3{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" "3{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\sec }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489419 :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" "-4{\\cot }^{2}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489422 :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" "-4{\\cot }^{2}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489426 :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" "4\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{\\sec }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489430 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(2x-1).$" "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={\\cot }^{4}⁡(2x-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186489430 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(2x-1))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8{cot}^{3}⁡(2x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-8{cot}^{3}⁡(2(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(2(0)-1) \\\\  & =-8{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-8(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =8{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{\\csc }^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{\\csc }^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{\\csc }^{2}⁡(2x-1))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8{\\cot }^{3}⁡(2x-1){\\csc }^{2}⁡(2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-8{\\cot }^{3}⁡(2(0)-1){\\csc }^{2}⁡(2(0)-1) \\\\  & =-8{\\cot }^{3}⁡(-1){\\csc }^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-8(-{\\cot }^{3}⁡1){\\csc }^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =8{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489431 :answer/value "2{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" "2{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\sec }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489432 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(3x-1).$" "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={\\cot }^{4}⁡(3x-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186489432 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=3x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(3x-1))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12{cot}^{3}⁡(3x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(3x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-12{cot}^{3}⁡(3(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(3(0)-1) \\\\  & =-12{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-12(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =12{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=3x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{\\csc }^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{\\csc }^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x-1))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12{\\cot }^{3}⁡(3x-1){\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-12{\\cot }^{3}⁡(3(0)-1){\\csc }^{2}⁡(3(0)-1) \\\\  & =-12{\\cot }^{3}⁡(-1){\\csc }^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-12(-{\\cot }^{3}⁡1){\\csc }^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =12{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489434 :answer/value "12{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" "12{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489436 :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" "-4{\\cot }^{2}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489442 :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" "-4{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1"] [:db/cas 17592186489443 :answer/value "2x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" "2x{\\sin }^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186489448 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(1x-1).$" "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={\\cot }^{4}⁡(1x-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186489448 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=1x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(1x-1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4{cot}^{3}⁡(1x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(1x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-4{cot}^{3}⁡(1(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(1(0)-1) \\\\  & =-4{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-4(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=1x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{\\csc }^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{\\csc }^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{\\csc }^{2}⁡(1x-1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4{\\cot }^{3}⁡(1x-1){\\csc }^{2}⁡(1x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-4{\\cot }^{3}⁡(1(0)-1){\\csc }^{2}⁡(1(0)-1) \\\\  & =-4{\\cot }^{3}⁡(-1){\\csc }^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-4(-{\\cot }^{3}⁡1){\\csc }^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =4{\\cot }^{3}⁡1{\\csc }^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186489449 :answer/value "6x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" "6x{\\sin }^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186489526 :answer/value "sup(A)=\\sqrt{5}\\in A" "\\sup (A)=\\sqrt{5}\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186489555 :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{13}{3}\\in A" "\\sup (A)=\\frac{13}{3}\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186489566 :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=-1\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186489593 :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\in A" "\\sup (A)=1\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186489601 :answer/value "inf(A)=0\\in A" "\\inf (A)=0\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186489620 :answer/value "sup(A)=1\\notin A" "\\sup (A)=1\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186489636 :answer/value "$sup(A)$ does not exist" "$\\sup (A)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186489709 :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\infty " "\\inf (A)=-\\infty "] [:db/cas 17592186489752 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}" "{\\log }_{2}⁡3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186489831 :answer/value "sup(A)=5\\in A" "\\sup (A)=5\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186489944 :answer/value "$sup(A)$ does not exist" "$\\sup (A)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186489976 :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\in A" "\\inf (A)=-1\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186489987 :answer/value "inf(A)=0\\notin A" "\\inf (A)=0\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186489989 :answer/value "sup(A)=5\\notin A" "\\sup (A)=5\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186490001 :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{13}{3}\\notin A" "\\sup (A)=\\frac{13}{3}\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186490028 :answer/value "sup(A)=\\sqrt{5}\\notin A" "\\sup (A)=\\sqrt{5}\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186490216 :question/problem "What is the infimum of the set $C=\\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}?$\n\n$inf(C)=$\n{{field-1}}" "What is the infimum of the set $C=\\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}?$\n\n$\\inf (C)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186490216 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a set $C$ is the greatest lower bound of $C.$\n\nIf $C$ is a finite set, the infimum of $C$ equals its minimum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(C) & =min\\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The infimum of a set $C$ is the greatest lower bound of $C.$\n\nIf $C$ is a finite set, the infimum of $C$ equals its minimum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\inf (C) & =\\min \\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490221 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/1e/1ea2ed214496ed38ae605e9ac64bebd781a0d5529561fce44d98cca6839d6fbb.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\ge 5$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x<5$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $5-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}$ is greater than $x$ for any $x<5.$\n\nTherefore, $[5,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[5,\\infty )=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that $5$ is *not* the maximum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no maximum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=5\\notin A.\n$$" "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/1e/1ea2ed214496ed38ae605e9ac64bebd781a0d5529561fce44d98cca6839d6fbb.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\ge 5$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x<5$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $5-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}$ is greater than $x$ for any $x<5.$\n\nTherefore, $[5,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\min [5,\\infty )=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that $5$ is *not* the maximum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no maximum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=5\\notin A.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490225 :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{3}{x{\\log }_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490225 :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{1}{{log}_{5}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u={\\log }_{5}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{3}{x{\\log }_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{1}{{\\log }_{5}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490234 :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot n:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot n:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$\\sup (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186490234 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{1+1}\\cdot 1 & =1 \\\\ (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot 2 & =-2 \\\\ (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot 3 & =3 \\\\ (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot 4 & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{1+1}\\cdot 1 & =1 \\\\ (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot 2 & =-2 \\\\ (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot 3 & =3 \\\\ (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot 4 & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sup (A) & =\\max \\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490242 :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{\\log }_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490242 :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{{log}_{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u={\\log }_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{\\log }_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{{\\log }_{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490258 :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{\\ln }^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490258 :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x).$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡8$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {(-{u}^{-1})|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x).$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡8$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{\\ln }^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {(-{u}^{-1})|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490259 :question/problem "Consider the set $A=[1,\\sqrt{5}].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(A).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." "Consider the set $A=[1,\\sqrt{5}].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $\\sup (A).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}."] [:db/cas 17592186490259 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{5}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{5}$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min\\,[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{5}$ is also the maximum of $A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $A$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(A)=\\sqrt{5}\\in A.$" "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{5}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{5}$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\min \\,[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{5}$ is also the maximum of $A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $A$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sup (A)=\\sqrt{5}\\in A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186490270 :question/problem "Consider the set $A=(-\\infty ,-1).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(A)$ and $sup(A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(A)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(A)$ {{field-6}}." "Consider the set $A=(-\\infty ,-1).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $\\inf (A)$ and $\\sup (A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-3}}, we have $\\inf (A)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-5}}, we have $\\sup (A)$ {{field-6}}."] [:db/cas 17592186490270 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $A$ is unbounded below, $A$ has no lower bounds, and $inf(A)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge -1$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[-1,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<-1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(A)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(A)=-1.$" "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $A$ is unbounded below, $A$ has no lower bounds, and $\\inf (A)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge -1$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[-1,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<-1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\min [-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $\\inf (A)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $\\sup (A)=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186490413 :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}\\tanh \\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right)\\sech \\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right) + C" "\\frac{6}{5}\\tanh \\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sech}  \\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490415 :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}\\csch^{2} \\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right) + C" "-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{csch} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490418 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(\\sech 3 - \\sech 2)" "\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sech}  3 - \\operatorname{sech}  2)"] [:db/cas 17592186490420 :answer/value "8(\\sech 2 - \\sech 8)" "8(\\operatorname{sech}  2 - \\operatorname{sech}  8)"] [:db/cas 17592186490421 :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\tanh \\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)\\sech \\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right) + C" "\\frac{3}{5}\\tanh \\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sech}  \\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490425 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\sech^{2} \\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\operatorname{sech} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490425 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{4x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\sech^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sech^2\\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{5}{4}\\int\\sech^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{4}\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{4}\\tanh\\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$." "Let $u=\\frac{4x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2\\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{5}{4}\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{4}\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{4}\\tanh\\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$."] [:db/cas 17592186490428 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\sech 3x\\tanh 3x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sech}  3x\\tanh 3x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490428 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=3$; when $x=2$, $u=6$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\sech u=-\\sech u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\sech(3x)\\tanh(3x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{3}^{6}\\sech u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{3}[-\\sech u]_{3}^{6} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{3}(\\sech 3-\\sech 6).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\sech u$ decreases for $u>0$." "Let $u=3x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=3$; when $x=2$, $u=6$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\operatorname{sech}  u=-\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\operatorname{sech} (3x)\\tanh(3x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{3}^{6}\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{3}[-\\operatorname{sech}  u]_{3}^{6} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sech}  3-\\operatorname{sech}  6).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\operatorname{sech}  u$ decreases for $u>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186490429 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(\\sech 6 - \\sech 12)" "\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sech}  6 - \\operatorname{sech}  12)"] [:db/cas 17592186490430 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\sech^{2} \\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\operatorname{sech} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490430 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{3x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\sech^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sech^2\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{5}{3}\\int\\sech^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{3}\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{3}\\tanh\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$." "Let $u=\\frac{3x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{5}{3}\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{3}\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{3}\\tanh\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$."] [:db/cas 17592186490433 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\tanh \\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right)\\sech \\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right) + C" "\\frac{1}{5}\\tanh \\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sech}  \\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490434 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(\\sech 4 - \\sech 2)" "\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sech}  4 - \\operatorname{sech}  2)"] [:db/cas 17592186490435 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}(\\sech 7 - \\sech 2)" "\\frac{1}{7}(\\operatorname{sech}  7 - \\operatorname{sech}  2)"] [:db/cas 17592186490436 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\sech^{2} \\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\operatorname{sech} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490436 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{1x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=5\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\sech^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sech^2\\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=5\\int\\sech^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=5\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=5\\tanh\\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$." "Let $u=\\frac{1x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=5\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2\\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=5\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=5\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=5\\tanh\\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$."] [:db/cas 17592186490437 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\sech 6x\\tanh 6x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sech}  6x\\tanh 6x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490437 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=6x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=6$; when $x=2$, $u=12$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\sech u=-\\sech u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\sech(6x)\\tanh(6x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{6}\\int_{6}^{12}\\sech u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{6}[-\\sech u]_{6}^{12} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{6}(\\sech 6-\\sech 12).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\sech u$ decreases for $u>0$." "Let $u=6x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=6$; when $x=2$, $u=12$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\operatorname{sech}  u=-\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\operatorname{sech} (6x)\\tanh(6x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{6}\\int_{6}^{12}\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{6}[-\\operatorname{sech}  u]_{6}^{12} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sech}  6-\\operatorname{sech}  12).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\operatorname{sech}  u$ decreases for $u>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186490438 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(\\sech 4 - \\sech 8)" "\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sech}  4 - \\operatorname{sech}  8)"] [:db/cas 17592186490442 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(\\sech 8 - \\sech 16)" "\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sech}  8 - \\operatorname{sech}  16)"] [:db/cas 17592186490443 :answer/value "-1\\csch^{2} \\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right) + C" "-1\\operatorname{csch} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490445 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(\\sech 3 - \\sech 6)" "\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sech}  3 - \\operatorname{sech}  6)"] [:db/cas 17592186490446 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\sech 4x\\tanh 4x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sech}  4x\\tanh 4x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490446 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=4x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=4$; when $x=2$, $u=8$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\sech u=-\\sech u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\sech(4x)\\tanh(4x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{4}^{8}\\sech u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}[-\\sech u]_{4}^{8} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}(\\sech 4-\\sech 8).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\sech u$ decreases for $u>0$." "Let $u=4x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=4$; when $x=2$, $u=8$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\operatorname{sech}  u=-\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\operatorname{sech} (4x)\\tanh(4x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{4}^{8}\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}[-\\operatorname{sech}  u]_{4}^{8} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sech}  4-\\operatorname{sech}  8).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\operatorname{sech}  u$ decreases for $u>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186490448 :answer/value "7(\\sech 2 - \\sech 7)" "7(\\operatorname{sech}  2 - \\operatorname{sech}  7)"] [:db/cas 17592186490449 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\sech 7x\\tanh 7x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sech}  7x\\tanh 7x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490449 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=7x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=7$; when $x=2$, $u=14$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\sech u=-\\sech u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\sech(7x)\\tanh(7x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{7}^{14}\\sech u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{7}[-\\sech u]_{7}^{14} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{7}(\\sech 7-\\sech 14).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\sech u$ decreases for $u>0$." "Let $u=7x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=7$; when $x=2$, $u=14$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\operatorname{sech}  u=-\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\operatorname{sech} (7x)\\tanh(7x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{7}^{14}\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{7}[-\\operatorname{sech}  u]_{7}^{14} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{7}(\\operatorname{sech}  7-\\operatorname{sech}  14).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\operatorname{sech}  u$ decreases for $u>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186490452 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(\\sech 6 - \\sech 2)" "\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sech}  6 - \\operatorname{sech}  2)"] [:db/cas 17592186490454 :answer/value "1\\tanh \\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right)\\sech \\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right) + C" "1\\tanh \\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sech}  \\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490455 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\sech 8x\\tanh 8x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sech}  8x\\tanh 8x\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490455 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=8x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=8$; when $x=2$, $u=16$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\sech u=-\\sech u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\sech(8x)\\tanh(8x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{8}\\int_{8}^{16}\\sech u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{8}[-\\sech u]_{8}^{16} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{8}(\\sech 8-\\sech 16).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\sech u$ decreases for $u>0$." "Let $u=8x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. When $x=1$, $u=8$; when $x=2$, $u=16$.\n\nUsing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\operatorname{sech}  u=-\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^2\\operatorname{sech} (8x)\\tanh(8x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{1}{8}\\int_{8}^{16}\\operatorname{sech}  u\\tanh u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{8}[-\\operatorname{sech}  u]_{8}^{16} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sech}  8-\\operatorname{sech}  16).\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[1,2]$, and the result is positive because $\\operatorname{sech}  u$ decreases for $u>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186490456 :answer/value "3(\\sech 2 - \\sech 3)" "3(\\operatorname{sech}  2 - \\operatorname{sech}  3)"] [:db/cas 17592186490459 :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}\\csch^{2} \\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right) + C" "-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{csch} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490461 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\sech^{2} \\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\operatorname{sech} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490461 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{5x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=1\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\sech^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sech^2\\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=1\\int\\sech^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=1\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=1\\tanh\\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$." "Let $u=\\frac{5x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=1\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2\\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=1\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=1\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=1\\tanh\\left(\\frac{5x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$."] [:db/cas 17592186490462 :answer/value "4(\\sech 2 - \\sech 4)" "4(\\operatorname{sech}  2 - \\operatorname{sech}  4)"] [:db/cas 17592186490464 :answer/value "-5\\csch^{2} \\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right) + C" "-5\\operatorname{csch} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{1x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490467 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}(\\sech 7 - \\sech 14)" "\\frac{1}{7}(\\operatorname{sech}  7 - \\operatorname{sech}  14)"] [:db/cas 17592186490468 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\sech^{2} \\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\int\\operatorname{sech} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490468 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{6x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\sech^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sech^2\\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{5}{6}\\int\\sech^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{6}\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{6}\\tanh\\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$." "Let $u=\\frac{6x}{5}$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$.\n\nUsing the substitution and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\tanh u=\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u$,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2\\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\frac{5}{6}\\int\\operatorname{sech} ^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{6}\\tanh u+C \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{6}\\tanh\\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right)+C.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe argument is linear and the integrand is defined for every real $x$."] [:db/cas 17592186490469 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(\\sech 8 - \\sech 2)" "\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sech}  8 - \\operatorname{sech}  2)"] [:db/cas 17592186490472 :answer/value "6(\\sech 2 - \\sech 6)" "6(\\operatorname{sech}  2 - \\operatorname{sech}  6)"] [:db/cas 17592186490473 :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\tanh \\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right)\\sech \\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right) + C" "\\frac{4}{5}\\tanh \\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sech}  \\left(\\frac{4x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490478 :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}\\csch^{2} \\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right) + C" "-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csch} ^{2} \\left(\\frac{6x}{5}\\right) + C"] [:db/cas 17592186490484 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={sin}^{4}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." "Given that $f(x)={\\sin }^{4}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186490484 :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{3}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =4{\\sin }^{3}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490485 :answer/value "-6{sin}^{5}⁡x" "-6{\\sin }^{5}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186490486 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{6}⁡(x).$" "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={\\cos }^{6}⁡(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186490486 :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{6},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=6{u}^{5}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{u}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-6{cos}^{5}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{6},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=6{u}^{5}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{u}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-6{\\cos }^{5}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490487 :answer/value "6{cos}^{5}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" "6{\\cos }^{5}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186490489 :answer/value "6{cos}^{5}⁡x" "6{\\cos }^{5}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186490490 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-2{cos}^{6}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." "Given that $f(x)=-2{\\cos }^{6}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186490490 :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))^{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{u}^{6},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-12{u}^{5},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-12{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =12{cos}^{5}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))^{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{u}^{6},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-12{u}^{5},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-12{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =12{\\cos }^{5}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186490494 :answer/value "-6{cos}^{5}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" "-6{\\cos }^{5}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186490496 :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{sin}^{5}⁡x" "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{\\sin }^{5}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186490571 :answer/value "sup(D)=\\sqrt{3}\\notin D" "\\sup (D)=\\sqrt{3}\\notin D"] [:db/cas 17592186490578 :answer/value "sup(D)=4\\notin D" "\\sup (D)=4\\notin D"] [:db/cas 17592186490587 :answer/value "inf(B)=\\pi \\in B" "\\inf (B)=\\pi \\in B"] [:db/cas 17592186490598 :answer/value "inf(B)=\\pi \\notin B" "\\inf (B)=\\pi \\notin B"] [:db/cas 17592186490604 :answer/value "sup(A)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\in A" "\\sup (A)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186490621 :answer/value "inf(B)=4\\notin B" "\\inf (B)=4\\notin B"] [:db/cas 17592186490680 :answer/value "$inf(B)$ does not exist" "$\\inf (B)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186490704 :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\in A" "\\sup (A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\in A"] [:db/cas 17592186490738 :answer/value "inf(B)=4\\in B" "\\inf (B)=4\\in B"] [:db/cas 17592186490760 :answer/value "sup(A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\notin A" "\\sup (A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186490933 :answer/value "$sup(A)$ does not exist" "$\\sup (A)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186490934 :answer/value "$sup(D)$ does not exist" "$\\sup (D)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186490960 :answer/value "sup(D)=4\\in D" "\\sup (D)=4\\in D"] [:db/cas 17592186490962 :answer/value "sup(D)=\\sqrt{3}\\in D" "\\sup (D)=\\sqrt{3}\\in D"] [:db/cas 17592186490989 :answer/value "sup(A)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\notin A" "\\sup (A)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\notin A"] [:db/cas 17592186491114 :question/problem "Consider the set $B=(\\pi ,4).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $inf(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." "Consider the set $B=(\\pi ,4).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $\\inf (B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}."] [:db/cas 17592186491114 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le \\pi $ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>\\pi $ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,\\pi ]=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\pi \\notin B.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le \\pi \\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(B)=\\pi \\notin B.$" "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le \\pi $ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>\\pi $ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\n\\inf (B)=\\max (-\\infty ,\\pi ]=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\pi \\notin B.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le \\pi \\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\inf (B)=\\pi \\notin B.$"] [:db/cas 17592186491137 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1}},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/69/690c0466ffac72597e9378539d1540d6baaa49c7cbe77bee8cc83ab00f065635.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{2}=(-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2+1}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{2}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{2}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\infty )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\in A.\n$$" "The supremum of $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1}},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/69/690c0466ffac72597e9378539d1540d6baaa49c7cbe77bee8cc83ab00f065635.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\max (A)={a}_{2}=(-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2+1}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{2}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{2}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\min [\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\infty )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sup (A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\in A.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491140 :question/problem "Consider the set $B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+1}:n\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $B?$\n\n$inf(B)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the set $B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+1}:n\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $B?$\n\n$\\inf (B)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186491140 :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a set $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the infimum of $B$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=0,1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}+1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nB=\\{1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(B) & =min\\{1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}=\\frac{1}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The infimum of a set $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the infimum of $B$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=0,1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}+1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nB=\\{1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\inf (B) & =\\min \\{1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{10},\\,\\frac{1}{17}\\}=\\frac{1}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186491151 :question/problem "Consider the set $D=[\\sqrt{3},4).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(D).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $D$ is the {{field-1}} of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." "Consider the set $D=[\\sqrt{3},4).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $\\sup (D).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $D$ is the {{field-1}} of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}."] [:db/cas 17592186491151 :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $D$ is the smallest upper bound of $D.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $D$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge 4$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $D.$ Thus, the set $[4,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $D.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<4$ is **not** an upper bound of $D.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $D$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<4.$\n\nTherefore, $[4,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $D,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(D)=min[4,\\infty )=4.\n$$\n\nNotice that $4\\notin D.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $D$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge 4\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(D)=4\\notin D.$" "The supremum of a set $D$ is the smallest upper bound of $D.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $D$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge 4$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $D.$ Thus, the set $[4,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $D.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<4$ is **not** an upper bound of $D.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $D$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<4.$\n\nTherefore, $[4,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $D,$ and\n\n$$\n\\sup (D)=\\min [4,\\infty )=4.\n$$\n\nNotice that $4\\notin D.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $D$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge 4\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sup (D)=4\\notin D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186491984 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)<0$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is negative-definite" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $\\det (A)<0$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is negative-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186491984 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${a}_{11}=1,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${a}_{11}=1,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186492066 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of the osculating circle: \n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the radius of the osculating circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of the osculating circle: \n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\cos }^{2}⁡2t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the radius of the osculating circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492836 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186492837 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-18,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{min}=-18.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-18:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-18)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+18\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-18)I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-18,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{min}=-18.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-18:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-18)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+18\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-18)I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493486 :answer/value "(z-1){sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " "(z-1){\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186493746 :answer/value "(z-1)^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " "(z-1)^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186493919 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186493919 :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So, we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3/2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{\\,3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we select the identity that contains a ${\\tan }^{2}⁡x$ term. So, we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-1={\\tan }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}{{\\tan }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3/2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{\\,3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186493961 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{\\tan }^{2}⁡x+1}{{\\sec }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186493961 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{\\tan }^{2}⁡x+1}{{\\sec }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{\\sec }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sec }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186495516 :answer/value "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ$ v" "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ$ v"] [:db/cas 17592186495527 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }