}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{\\sin }^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ"] [:db/cas 17592186495829 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ"] [:db/cas 17592186495854 :answer/value "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ$ v" "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ$ v"] [:db/cas 17592186495912 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}{\\sin }^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ"] [:db/cas 17592186495936 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ"] [:db/cas 17592186495946 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ"] [:db/cas 17592186496227 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/d2/d2b9c6f3541c47f37ad7f2ba52d739787e39600ec71fe3530f0ad2846c24880b.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/d2/d2b9c6f3541c47f37ad7f2ba52d739787e39600ec71fe3530f0ad2846c24880b.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496299 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{5}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496307 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{\\rho }^{5}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{32}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{3}{\\cos }^{3}⁡ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{\\rho }^{5}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{32}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496318 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =8\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/04/0475e2e272a30318dc4cb31457539a312544b6e14d7e6a8ed3f911d41404b575.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =8\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/04/0475e2e272a30318dc4cb31457539a312544b6e14d7e6a8ed3f911d41404b575.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496747 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186496784 :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186496976 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186497127 :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186497197 :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186497344 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x(1-{y}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x(1-{y}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497372 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{cos}^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({cos}^{2}⁡(t)-(-{cos}^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)-(-{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497410 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497410 :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498488 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=6.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{3}:=4{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $\\det (A)=6.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{3}:=4{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $\\det (B)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186498488 :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=4{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-4det(A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=4{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =4\\cdot (-\\det (A)) \\\\  & =-4\\det (A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498522 :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of invertible matrices." "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $\\det (Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of invertible matrices."] [:db/cas 17592186498522 :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices." "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $\\det (Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices."] [:db/cas 17592186498777 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186498839 :answer/value "xy{sin}^{2}⁡y" "xy{\\sin }^{2}⁡y"] [:db/cas 17592186499502 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{({\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{({\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499546 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501234 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{27}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}} \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={3}^{2}\\cdot 3=27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡3t+{27}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}} \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={3}^{2}\\cdot 3=27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502079 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(r),{1}_{B}(r)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0,$ we conclude that $r$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(r),{1}_{B}(r)\\} \\\\  & =\\min \\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0,$ we conclude that $r$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186502127 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(2),{1}_{B}(2)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0,$ we conclude that $2$ does not belong to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(2),{1}_{B}(2)\\} \\\\  & =\\max \\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0,$ we conclude that $2$ does not belong to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186503147 :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+(2x+1){sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}" "\\frac{4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+(2x+1){\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186503278 :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}" "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}"] [:db/cas 17592186503310 :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+1){sinh}^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{4}⁡x}" "\\frac{(2x+1){\\sinh }^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{4}⁡x}"] [:db/cas 17592186503389 :answer/value "\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{4}⁡x}" "\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{4}⁡x}"] [:db/cas 17592186503419 :answer/value "\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}" "\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186503664 :question/problem "Simplify $tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${x}^{3}+1$ |" "Simplify $\\tanh \\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\tanh \\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${x}^{3}+1$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186503664 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tanh\\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}-1}{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}-1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tanh \\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}-1}{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}-1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503685 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503709 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $g(x)=\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}.$" "Find the derivative of $g(x)=\\frac{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186503709 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $g(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={sinh}^{2}⁡x$ and $v(x)=2x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sinh}^{2}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2x+1)^{′}}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2)}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, the expression for $g(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={\\sinh }^{2}⁡x$ and $v(x)=2x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (2x+1)-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2x+1)^{′}}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\cdot (2x+1)-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2)}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503712 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-1.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503717 :question/problem "Simplify $tanh\\,\\left(\\frac{k}{3}\\right)$ in terms of $e$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$tanh\\,\\left(\\frac{k}{3}\\right)=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${e}^{2k/3}+1$ |" "Simplify $\\tanh \\,\\left(\\frac{k}{3}\\right)$ in terms of $e$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\tanh \\,\\left(\\frac{k}{3}\\right)=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${e}^{2k/3}+1$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186503717 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tanh\\,\\left(\\frac{k}{3}\\right) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(k/3)}-1}{{e}^{2(k/3)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2k/3}-1}{{e}^{2k/3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tanh \\,\\left(\\frac{k}{3}\\right) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(k/3)}-1}{{e}^{2(k/3)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2k/3}-1}{{e}^{2k/3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503737 :question/problem "Evaluate $sinh\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3/2}\\,-\n$$ {{field-1}} $)$" "Evaluate $\\sinh \\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3/2}\\,-\n$$ {{field-1}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186503737 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sinh\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3/2}-{e}^{-3/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sinh \\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3/2}-{e}^{-3/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504074 :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{\\sec }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186504153 :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" "$f(x)={\\cos }^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186504193 :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0$" "$f(x)={\\cos }^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186504218 :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" "$f(x)={\\cos }^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186504233 :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x-\\pi )$ and $b=\\pi $" "$f(x)={\\cos }^{2}⁡(x-\\pi )$ and $b=\\pi $"] [:db/cas 17592186504394 :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=0$" "$f(x)={\\cos }^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186504603 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k), & x\\ne -1, \\\\ 3, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡(2x+k), & x\\ne -1, \\\\ 3, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186504603 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -1}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(k-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(k-2) & =3 \\\\ k-2 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ k-2 & =125 \\\\ k & =127.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=127$ removes the point discontinuity." "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -1}{\\log }_{5}⁡(2x+k) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{5}⁡(k-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {\\log }_{5}⁡(k-2) & =3 \\\\ k-2 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ k-2 & =125 \\\\ k & =127.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=127$ removes the point discontinuity."] [:db/cas 17592186504619 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{\\log }_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\log }_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504635 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x)+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186504641 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {\\sec }^{2}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186504647 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k), & x\\ne 1, \\\\ 2, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$" "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡3(x-k), & x\\ne 1, \\\\ 2, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186504647 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡3(1-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡3(1-k) & =2 \\\\ 3(1-k) & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 1-k & =3 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=-2$ removes the point discontinuity." "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}{\\log }_{3}⁡3(x-k) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡3(1-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡3(1-k) & =2 \\\\ 3(1-k) & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 1-k & =3 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=-2$ removes the point discontinuity."] [:db/cas 17592186504655 :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}\\right){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{\\sec }^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}\\right){e}^{0}{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"] [:db/cas 17592186504709 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x)+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186505734 :question/worked-solution "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}\\right)}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50}{49}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50-49}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\ln \\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}\\right)}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50}{49}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50-49}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505745 :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain. We have $y\\in [0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the right-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & ={ln}^{2}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require real $y$ and $x<\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}$ for all $x,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}))}^{2}.\n$$" "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain. We have $y\\in [0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the right-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & ={\\ln }^{2}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require real $y$ and $x<\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}$ for all $x,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}))}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505772 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}{1-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,ln\\,\\left(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1+\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}{1-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\ln \\,\\left(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505780 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{\\sin }^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505780 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\n$$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\n$$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{\\sin }^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{4}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186505828 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{\\sin }^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186505828 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\n$$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{18}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\left(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\n$$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{\\sin }^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{4}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{18}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\left(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\right){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506509 :question/problem "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((ax+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(b{x}^{2}+cx)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}}((ax+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(b{x}^{2}+cx)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186506509 :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$" "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506513 :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((ax+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{\\cos }^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((ax+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186506513 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({cos}^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\left(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\left(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{\\cos }^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{\\cos }^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\cos }^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{\\cos }^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{\\cos }^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{\\cos }^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\left(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{\\cos }^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}\\left(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{\\cos }^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506543 :question/problem "Given that $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}={x}^{x}($\n{{field-1}}$+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+{sec}^{2}⁡(x))$" "Given that $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}={x}^{x}($\n{{field-1}}$+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+{\\sec }^{2}⁡(x))$"] [:db/cas 17592186506543 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}+{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}+{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {\\sec }^{2}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186506577 :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(ax+63-8({x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}(ax+63-8({x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186506577 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{8}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{6(x+8)+5(x+3)-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(x+3)(x+8)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{6x+48+5x+15-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=11+11+24=46.\n$$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\sin }^{8}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{6(x+8)+5(x+3)-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(x+3)(x+8)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{6x+48+5x+15-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{\\sin }^{8}⁡x}(11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=11+11+24=46.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186507452 :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL=\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "First, we let\n\n$$\nL=\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507525 :question/problem "Given that the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(cx-4), & \\frac{2}{3}<x\\le 1 \\\\ 2x-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at $x=1,$ find the value of $c.$" "Given that the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡(cx-4), & \\frac{2}{3}<x\\le 1 \\\\ 2x-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at $x=1,$ find the value of $c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186507525 :question/worked-solution "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(c(1)-4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(c-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(c-4) & =1 \\\\ c-4 & ={2}^{1} \\\\ c & =2+4 \\\\ c & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(c(1)-4) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(c-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(c-4) & =1 \\\\ c-4 & ={2}^{1} \\\\ c & =2+4 \\\\ c & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508036 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=f(x),\\,y(4)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(4)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=f(x),\\,y(4)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(4)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508036 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+2y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+2y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(4)=3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4) & =A{e}^{-2(4)}+{y}_{p}(4) \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{-8}+0 \\\\ A & =3{e}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(3{e}^{8}){e}^{-2x}=3{e}^{8-2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼3{e}^{8-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+2y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+2y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(4)=3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4) & =A{e}^{-2(4)}+{y}_{p}(4) \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{-8}+0 \\\\ A & =3{e}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(3{e}^{8}){e}^{-2x}=3{e}^{8-2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼3{e}^{8-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508605 :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186508660 :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186508802 :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186508963 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508963 :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186508977 :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $q(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂q}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂q}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂q}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂q}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $q$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂q}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂q}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $q(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂q}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂q}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂q}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂q}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $q$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂q}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂q}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186509900 :answer/value "3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " "3{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186510072 :answer/value "1+{sin}^{2}⁡u" "1+{\\sin }^{2}⁡u"] [:db/cas 17592186510209 :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " "3{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186510461 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y(u,v,w)=3v,\\,z(u,v,w)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y(u,v,w)=3v,\\,z(u,v,w)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510569 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\det (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186510573 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\det (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186510575 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\det (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186510576 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\det (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186510583 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{7} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{8}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{7} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{8}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\det (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186510611 :question/problem "Calculate $det(R)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 14 \\\\ -7 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(R)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (R)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 14 \\\\ -7 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\det (R)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186510611 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 14 \\\\ -7 & 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-8)(13)-(14)(-7) \\\\  & =-104+98 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 14 \\\\ -7 & 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-8)(13)-(14)(-7) \\\\  & =-104+98 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510612 :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 7 & -15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (A)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 7 & -15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\det (A)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186510612 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 7 & -15\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(7)(-15)-(-12)(7) \\\\  & =-105+84 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 7 & -15\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(7)(-15)-(-12)(7) \\\\  & =-105+84 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510613 :question/problem "Calculate $det(S)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(S)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (S)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\det (S)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186510613 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-9)(9)-(1)(9) \\\\  & =-81-9 \\\\  & =-90.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-9)(9)-(1)(9) \\\\  & =-81-9 \\\\  & =-90.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510727 :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccdb5bb029197875aaeebfc899dff4d25597b657e64ea95d1b34d09df0a37789.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" "![](images/cc/ccdb5bb029197875aaeebfc899dff4d25597b657e64ea95d1b34d09df0a37789.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\tan ⁡\\theta .$"] [:db/cas 17592186510727 :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af413350ef3c9edde0eebf49cb8f37c8b20791f4bb01e04adaacaa0c378d28a1.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{43}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43\\cdot 6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/af/af413350ef3c9edde0eebf49cb8f37c8b20791f4bb01e04adaacaa0c378d28a1.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\cos ⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sin ⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos ⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{43}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\cos ⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\cos ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\tan ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sin ⁡\\theta }{\\cos ⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43\\cdot 6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510730 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d04fe72eb2ad53b69837aaf402addd75d9b087225d70e876233e23092fc85764.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" "![](images/d0/d04fe72eb2ad53b69837aaf402addd75d9b087225d70e876233e23092fc85764.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\tan ⁡\\theta .$"] [:db/cas 17592186510730 :question/worked-solution "![](images/51/51778a4029b91dbbd51014a8f4a3e7e1fc772efe96058f41b21bbf408f113cce.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{18}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18\\cdot 7}}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{126}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/51/51778a4029b91dbbd51014a8f4a3e7e1fc772efe96058f41b21bbf408f113cce.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\cos ⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sin ⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos ⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{18}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\cos ⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\cos ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\tan ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sin ⁡\\theta }{\\cos ⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18\\cdot 7}}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{126}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186510733 :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2c66fc4d5fa2ac5b2d6263764cf1eaa4e845680561833c5bfa46743df3408d2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7},$ use the Pytha