[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,4.$  \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=\\max \\{-3,3\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511571 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3-5 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-10,-7,-12\\}=-12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-12,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3-5 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{-10,-7,-12\\}=-12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-12,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511572 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,-3\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{1,-3\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511577 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[2,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (2,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are$x=2,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$" "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[2,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (2,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are$x=2,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=\\max \\{-2,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511686 :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a6e00e08f331baf932661a341627dd6403ca5c33f17b5fe51f2343198c1b5f1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{7}{8},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" "![](images/9a/9a6e00e08f331baf932661a341627dd6403ca5c33f17b5fe51f2343198c1b5f1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{7}{8},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin ⁡\\theta .$"] [:db/cas 17592186511686 :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/608cde6d839f956b950438c50f54338fb7ec7b816ffc21ac38515dbf79a9f980.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{7}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$" "![](images/60/608cde6d839f956b950438c50f54338fb7ec7b816ffc21ac38515dbf79a9f980.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\cos ⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sin ⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{7}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos ⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin ⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\sin ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos ⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sin ⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin ⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511687 :question/problem "![](images/22/2242f7a5c4b3386e8f493799e71645145e9290fa5eaad66f986931d9e38006e2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" "![](images/22/2242f7a5c4b3386e8f493799e71645145e9290fa5eaad66f986931d9e38006e2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .$"] [:db/cas 17592186511687 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a6/a6a3acf1948838a3d1d8f0effe0e1cdb30282b289f89009cbbcac716b4b73700.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{38}{25}.$" "![](images/a6/a6a3acf1948838a3d1d8f0effe0e1cdb30282b289f89009cbbcac716b4b73700.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\cos ⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sin ⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{38}{25}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511701 :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0cdc84ef38e2c2074bdab95458a97452209858b74a370b65bd7f393c1bde908.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" "![](images/e0/e0cdc84ef38e2c2074bdab95458a97452209858b74a370b65bd7f393c1bde908.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .$"] [:db/cas 17592186511701 :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69ab1b2e23a2815343db2b914204aa5864e73254dd1aa22d501c08b8ae155714.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{17}{49}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{34}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{64}{49}.$" "![](images/69/69ab1b2e23a2815343db2b914204aa5864e73254dd1aa22d501c08b8ae155714.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\cos ⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sin ⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{17}{49}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{34}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{64}{49}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511705 :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd7cf164aa9ac00697292aabf03d616b62b216014ac00a0f36e9194ad7685ce2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" "![](images/bd/bd7cf164aa9ac00697292aabf03d616b62b216014ac00a0f36e9194ad7685ce2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .$"] [:db/cas 17592186511705 :question/worked-solution "![](images/df/df6444faa947e137efc95beeb24c59e8e685830096bf4019fef776abd87c4bff.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{6}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}.$" "![](images/df/df6444faa947