n{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{13}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{26}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{24}{25}.$" "![](images/09/0961629419accd50af36ce88aecafe33ea264d278d18d6f71c4aa4d76c06be97.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\cos ⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sin ⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin ⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\left(\\frac{13}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{26}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{24}{25}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186512177 :answer/value "{log}_{1/4}⁡16=2" "{\\log }_{1/4}⁡16=2"] [:db/cas 17592186512249 :answer/value "{log}_{1/4}⁡2=16" "{\\log }_{1/4}⁡2=16"] [:db/cas 17592186512250 :answer/value "{log}_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}" "{\\log }_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186512321 :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡2=16" "{\\log }_{4}⁡2=16"] [:db/cas 17592186512574 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡16=\\frac{1}{4}" "{\\log }_{2}⁡16=\\frac{1}{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186512879 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-8) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,-10\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-8) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{-2,-10\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186512901 :question/problem "Consider two sets of $16$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{16}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=188,\\,{S}_{yy}=354,\\,{S}_{xy}=212.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." "Consider two sets of $16$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{16}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=188,\\,{S}_{yy}=354,\\,{S}_{xy}=212.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186512901 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=212.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{212}{16} \\\\  & =13.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=212.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{212}{16} \\\\  & =13.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186512919 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-2,4],$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/19/1996a5ab1052a63a9513ed57a6a74ea82ad5994b62288cb04e4df02dac7589b6.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-2) & =6 \\\\ h(0) & =h(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(3) & =h(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8+3 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ h(4) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11+(-1) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $h$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{6,8,11,10\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{min}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-2,4],$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/19/1996a5ab1052a63a9513ed57a6a74ea82ad5994b62288cb04e4df02dac7589b6.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-2) & =6 \\\\ h(0) & =h(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(3) & =h(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8+3 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ h(4) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11+(-1) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $h$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{6,8,11,10\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{min}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186512922 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,6,0\\}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=0,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{1,6,0\\}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=0,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186512923 :question/problem "Consider two sets of $10$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{10}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=5,\\,{S}_{yy}=7,\\,{S}_{xy}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." "Consider two sets of $10$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{10}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=5,\\,{S}_{yy}=7,\\,{S}_{xy}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186512923 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=6.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=6.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186512930 :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$" "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{{field-2}}\\cdot \\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186512930 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n} &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}\\right)\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot cos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n} &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}\\right)\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186512947 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d2/d20759e0c246f1fad5e0a36e495302c6b8de0e23a5f8ed25bc291147750a1342.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1)+4.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,3,7.5\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d2/d20759e0c246f1fad5e0a36e495302c6b8de0e23a5f8ed25bc291147750a1342.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1)+4.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{4,3,7.5\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186512950 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6-9 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,5,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6-9 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{-1,5,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186512951 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.24$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.24}{0.02}=12.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈12 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(12) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.585… \\\\ p & ≈2.585….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.24$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.24}{0.02}=12.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈12 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{\\log }_{2}⁡(12) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.585… \\\\ p & ≈2.585….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186512953 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2,4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ea/ea837252a62313eae506ef3d101c0b70121f6380694a49c2f54e1a19702949a5.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3) \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3)+0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,5,2,2.5\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2,4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ea/ea837252a62313eae506ef3d101c0b70121f6380694a49c2f54e1a19702949a5.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3) \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3)+0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{4,5,2,2.5\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186513531 :answer/value "{log}_{6}⁡2=x" "{\\log }_{6}⁡2=x"] [:db/cas 17592186513533 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡64=x" "{\\log }_{2}⁡64=x"] [:db/cas 17592186513546 :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{sech}^{2}⁡x" "-6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186513553 :answer/value "{log}_{9}⁡81=\\frac{1}{2}" "{\\log }_{9}⁡81=\\frac{1}{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186513554 :answer/value "{log}_{1}⁡0=3" "{\\log }_{1}⁡0=3"] [:db/cas 17592186513563 :answer/value "2x{e}^{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{tanh}^{2}⁡{x}^{2}" "2x{e}^{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{\\tanh }^{2}⁡{x}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186513573 :answer/value "{log}_{1/3}⁡(1.587)≈4" "{\\log }_{1/3}⁡(1.587)≈4"] [:db/cas 17592186513574 :answer/value "{log}_{1/16}⁡16=0" "{\\log }_{1/16}⁡16=0"] [:db/cas 17592186513577 :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}" "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}"] [:db/cas 17592186513578 :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(1.587)≈\\frac{1}{3}" "{\\log }_{4}⁡(1.587)≈\\frac{1}{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186513580 :answer/value "{log}_{1.414}⁡(2)≈\\frac{1}{2}" "{\\log }_{1.414}⁡(2)≈\\frac{1}{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186513582 :answer/value "{log}_{1/16}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=0" "{\\log }_{1/16}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)=0"] [:db/cas 17592186513586 :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x" "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{