h }^{2}⁡\\theta +C"] [:db/cas 17592186515256 :answer/value "\\frac{2x(\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x-x{tanh}^{2}⁡2x)}{{sech}^{2}⁡2x}" "\\frac{2x(\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x-x{\\tanh }^{2}⁡2x)}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡2x}"] [:db/cas 17592186515280 :answer/value "2{x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-x{csch}^{2}⁡x)" "2{x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)"] [:db/cas 17592186515285 :answer/value "-{x}^{3}{csch}^{2}⁡2x" "-{x}^{3}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡2x"] [:db/cas 17592186515288 :answer/value "4{csch}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" "4{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186515291 :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+2x{coth}^{2}⁡x)" "-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+2x{\\coth }^{2}⁡x)"] [:db/cas 17592186515297 :answer/value "-2x{csch}^{2}⁡({x}^{2}-1)" "-2x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡({x}^{2}-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186515319 :answer/value "-x{csch}^{2}⁡2x" "-x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡2x"] [:db/cas 17592186515330 :answer/value "2(\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x-2{x}^{2}{csch}^{2}⁡2x)" "2(\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x-2{x}^{2}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡2x)"] [:db/cas 17592186515337 :answer/value "3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-{sech}^{2}⁡x" "3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186515340 :answer/value "6{csch}^{2}⁡x" "6{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186515343 :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta +C" "{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta +C"] [:db/cas 17592186515362 :answer/value "2x{coth}^{2}⁡{x}^{2}" "2x{\\coth }^{2}⁡{x}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186515372 :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡\\theta +{csch}^{2}⁡\\theta +C" "{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡\\theta +C"] [:db/cas 17592186515379 :answer/value "2x(2\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+{x}^{2}{sech}^{2}⁡x)" "2x(2\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+{x}^{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x)"] [:db/cas 17592186515392 :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta +C" "{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta +C"] [:db/cas 17592186515401 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186515407 :answer/value "-4{csch}^{2}⁡3x" "-4{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡3x"] [:db/cas 17592186515524 :question/problem "$$\n\\int (3+2{sech}^{2}⁡w)\\,\\text{d}w=\n$$" "$$\n\\int (3+2{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡w)\\,\\text{d}w=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515524 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}w}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡w)={sech}^{2}⁡w\\,⇒\\,\\int {sech}^{2}⁡w\\,\\text{d}w=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡w+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3+2{sech}^{2}⁡w)\\,\\text{d}w & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}w+\\int 2{sech}^{2}⁡w\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =3\\int \\,\\text{d}w+2\\int {sech}^{2}⁡w\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =3w+2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡w+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}w}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡w)={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡w\\,⇒\\,\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡w\\,\\text{d}w=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡w+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3+2{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡w)\\,\\text{d}w & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}w+\\int 2{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡w\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =3\\int \\,\\text{d}w+2\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡w\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =3w+2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡w+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515545 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)^{′}=-{csch}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =4(-{csch}^{2}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4{csch}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(-4\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)^{′}=-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =4(-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(-4\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515547 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=2{x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(2{x}^{3})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡x+2{x}^{3}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡x+2{x}^{3}\\cdot (-{csch}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-2{x}^{3}{csch}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-x{csch}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=2{x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(2{x}^{3})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡x+2{x}^{3}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡x+2{x}^{3}\\cdot (-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-2{x}^{3}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515559 :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x},$ then ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{g(x)}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}.$ What is $g(x)?$" "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x},$ then ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{g(x)}{x{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}.$ What is $g(x)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186515559 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\overbrace{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+1)}^{g(x)}}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+1).$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\overbrace{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+1)}^{g(x)}}{x{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186515561 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{coth}⁡g,\\,g={x}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=-{csch}^{2}⁡g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-{csch}^{2}⁡g\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-2x{csch}^{2}⁡g \\\\  & =-2x{csch}^{2}⁡({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{coth}⁡g,\\,g={x}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡g\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-2x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡g \\\\  & =-2x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515565 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=2x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(2x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}+2x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}+2x\\cdot (-2x{csch}^{2}⁡{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}{csch}^{2}⁡{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}{csch}^{2}⁡{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=2x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(2x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}+2x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}+2x\\cdot (-2x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{coth}⁡{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515569 :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ then ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{g(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}.$ What is $g(x)?$" "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ then ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{g(x)}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}.$ What is $g(x)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186515569 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\overbrace{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{g(x)}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\overbrace{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{g(x)}}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186515575 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({x}^{2})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sech}⁡2x-{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x-{x}^{2}(-2\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)}{{sech}^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x(1+x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)}{{sech}^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x(1+x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({x}^{2})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sech}⁡2x-{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x-{x}^{2}(-2\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x(1+x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x(1+x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515590 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)^{′}=-{csch}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)^{′}=-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515591 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=2{sech}^{2}⁡x.$" "Find the derivative of $f(x)=2{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186515591 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=2{g}^{2},\\,g=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4g(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4{sech}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=2{g}^{2},\\,g=\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4g(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)(-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515598 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515598 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)=-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)=-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,⇒\\,\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515601 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=-3x{csch}^{2}⁡x.$" "Find the derivative of $f(x)=-3x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186515601 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=-3x$ and $v(x)={csch}^{2}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3x)}^{′}\\cdot {csch}^{2}⁡x+(-3x)\\cdot {({csch}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {csch}^{2}⁡x-3x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3{csch}^{2}⁡x+6x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x \\\\  & =3{csch}^{2}⁡x(2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=-3x$ and $v(x)={\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3x)}^{′}\\cdot {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x+(-3x)\\cdot {({\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x-3x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x+6x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x \\\\  & =3{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x(2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515609 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)^{′}=-{csch}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =6(-{csch}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{csch}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic reciprocal hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)^{′}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x,\\,(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)^{′}=-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =6(-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515740 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+2\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x + 2π)=-\\frac{4}{9}.$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan ⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x+2\\pi ) & =\\tan ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x + 2π)=-\\frac{4}{9}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186515743 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x - 7π)=-\\frac{9}{5}.$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan ⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =\\tan ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x - 7π)=-\\frac{9}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186515750 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x-4\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x - 4π)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan ⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x-4\\pi ) & =\\tan ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x - 4π)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186515751 :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=6,$ then $tan⁡(-x)=$" "If $\\tan ⁡x=6,$ then $\\tan ⁡(-x)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186515751 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515754 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x - 7π)=-\\frac{3}{5}.$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan ⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =\\tan ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x - 7π)=-\\frac{3}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186515768 :question/problem "If $tan⁡a=-3,$ then $tan⁡(-a)=$" "If $\\tan ⁡a=-3,$ then $\\tan ⁡(-a)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186515768 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-a) & =-tan⁡a \\\\  & =-(-3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-a) & =-\\tan ⁡a \\\\  & =-(-3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515776 :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-6,$ then $\\frac{2}{3}tan⁡(-x+2\\pi )+1=$" "If $\\tan ⁡x=-6,$ then $\\frac{2}{3}\\tan ⁡(-x+2\\pi )+1=$"] [:db/cas 17592186515776 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+2\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}tan⁡(-x+2\\pi )+1 & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3}+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan ⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x+2\\pi ) & =\\tan ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-(-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\tan ⁡(-x+2\\pi )+1 & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3}+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515778 :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-8,$ then $\\frac{1}{8}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+3=$" "If $\\tan ⁡x=-8,$ then $\\frac{1}{8}\\tan ⁡(-x-7\\pi )+3=$"] [:db/cas 17592186515778 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-8) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}tan⁡(-x-7\\pi )+3 & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 8+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8}+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan ⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x-7\\pi ) & =\\tan ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-(-8) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}\\tan ⁡(-x-7\\pi )+3 & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 8+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8}+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515793 :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=-7,$ then $\\frac{7}{8}tan⁡(-x+\\pi )+\\frac{3}{8}=$" "If $\\tan ⁡x=-7,$ then $\\frac{7}{8}\\tan ⁡(-x+\\pi )+\\frac{3}{8}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186515793 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-7) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}tan⁡(-x+\\pi )+\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{7}{8}\\cdot 7+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan ⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x+\\pi ) & =\\tan ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-(-7) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}\\tan ⁡(-x+\\pi )+\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{7}{8}\\cdot 7+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186515803 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $tan⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡(-x)=-tan⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+6\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $tan⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x + 6π)=-\\frac{7}{4}.$" "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan ⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan ⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan ⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan ⁡(-x)=-\\tan ⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x+6\\pi ) & =\\tan ⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan ⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡(-x) & =-\\tan ⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x + 6π)=-\\frac{7}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186516343 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" "\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}}"] [:db/cas 17592186516572 :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}" "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186516603 :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}" "\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186516768 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(1)=-3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(1)=-3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516768 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=-3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =A{e}^{-4(1)}+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ -3 & =A{e}^{-4}+0 \\\\ A & =-3{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(-3{e}^{4}){e}^{-4x}=-3{e}^{4-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-3{e}^{4-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)).\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=-3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =A{e}^{-4(1)}+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ -3 & =A{e}^{-4}+0 \\\\ A & =-3{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(-3{e}^{4}){e}^{-4x}=-3{e}^{4-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-3{e}^{4-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516774 :question/problem "The equation ${log}_{64}⁡4=\\frac{1}{3}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{b}=4.$ What is the value of $b?$" "The equation ${\\log }_{64}⁡4=\\frac{1}{3}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{b}=4.$ What is the value of $b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186516774 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{64}⁡4=\\frac{1}{3}$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=4,$ $b=64,$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${64}^{1/3}=4,$ which is also equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{64}=4.$ Consequently, $b=64.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${\\log }_{64}⁡4=\\frac{1}{3}$ with ${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=4,$ $b=64,$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${64}^{1/3}=4,$ which is also equivalent to $\\sqrt[3]{64}=4.$ Consequently, $b=64.$"] [:db/cas 17592186516782 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x-1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516788 :question/problem "If the equation ${log}_{9}⁡9=1$ is equivalent to ${b}^{1}=9,$  then $b=${{field-1}}." "If the equation ${\\log }_{9}⁡9=1$ is equivalent to ${b}^{1}=9,$  then $b=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186516788 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{9}⁡9=1$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=9,$ $b=9,$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${9}^{1}=9.$ Consequently, $b=9.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${\\log }_{9}⁡9=1$ with ${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=9,$ $b=9,$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${9}^{1}=9.$ Consequently, $b=9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186516793 :question/problem "Express the equation ${log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{125}\\right)=-3$ in exponential form. Write the exponential term on the left-hand side of the equation.\n\n{{field-1}}$=$\n{{field-2}}" "Express the equation ${\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{125}\\right)=-3$ in exponential form. Write the exponential term on the left-hand side of the equation.\n\n{{field-1}}$=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186516793 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{125}\\right)=-3$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{125},$ $b=5,$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{5}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{125}.\n$$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{125}\\right)=-3$ with ${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=\\frac{1}{125},$ $b=5,$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{5}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{125}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516810 :question/problem "The equation ${log}_{5}⁡25=2$ is equivalent to ${5}^{x}=25.$ Determine the value of $x.$" "The equation ${\\log }_{5}⁡25=2$ is equivalent to ${5}^{x}=25.$ Determine the value of $x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186516810 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{5}⁡25=2$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=25,$ $b=5,$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${5}^{2}=25.$ Consequently, $x=2.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${\\log }_{5}⁡25=2$ with ${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=25,$ $b=5,$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${5}^{2}=25.$ Consequently, $x=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186516819 :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $12$-periodic on $[-6,6]$, we have $L=6$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)\n$$" "Because $f$ is $12$-periodic on $[-6,6]$, we have $L=6$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\n\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516829 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516829 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-5y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-5y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-2.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{5(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ -2 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=-2{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-2{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-5y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-5y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-2.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{5(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ -2 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=-2{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-2{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516831 :question/problem "If $y=arsinh\\,(\\frac{x-3}{3}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" "If $y=\\operatorname{arsinh} \\,(\\frac{x-3}{3}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186516831 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(arsinh\\,(\\frac{x-3}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x-3}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(\\frac{x-3}{3})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-3)^{2}+9}{9}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-6x+9)+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x+18}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{arsinh} \\,(\\frac{x-3}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x-3}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(\\frac{x-3}{3})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-3)^{2}+9}{9}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-6x+9)+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x+18}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516834 :question/problem "If the equation ${log}_{2}⁡(128)=7$ is equivalent to ${b}^{7}=128,$  then $b=${{field-1}}." "If the equation ${\\log }_{2}⁡(128)=7$ is equivalent to ${b}^{7}=128,$  then $b=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186516834 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{2}⁡(128)=7$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=128,$ $b=2,$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${2}^{7}=128.$ Consequently,  $b=2.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${\\log }_{2}⁡(128)=7$ with ${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=128,$ $b=2,$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${2}^{7}=128.$ Consequently,  $b=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186516847 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $3$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$" "$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $3$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516847 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+5=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{5}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=3.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=3$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+5=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{5}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=3.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=3$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186516849 :question/problem "If the equation ${log}_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ is equivalent to ${b}^{-3}=a,$  then $a=${{field-1}}." "If the equation ${\\log }_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ is equivalent to ${b}^{-3}=a,$  then $a=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186516849 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=64,$ $b=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${log}_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ is equivalent to ${\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{-3}=64.$ Consequently, $a=64.$" "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${\\log }_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ with ${\\log }_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=64,$ $b=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${\\log }_{1/4}⁡64=-3$ is equivalent to ${\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{-3}=64.$ Consequently, $a=64.$"] [:db/cas 17592186518117 :question/problem "${log}_{13}⁡1=$" "${\\log }_{13}⁡1=$"] [:db/cas 17592186518117 :question/worked-solution "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=13.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{13}⁡1=0\n$$" "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=13.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{13}⁡1=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518147 :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+18y=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+18y=6{\\sin }^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?"] [:db/cas 17592186518147 :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=18\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $$\n\\displaystyle p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$" "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=18\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=6{\\sin }^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=6{\\sin }^{2}⁡pt=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $$\n\\displaystyle p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518150 :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)=$" "${\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186518150 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{27}$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{27}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}={3}^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=3$ and $x=-3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})=-3\n$$" "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{27}$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{27}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}={3}^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)={\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=3$ and $x=-3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})=-3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518154 :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{81}\\right)=$" "${\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{81}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186518154 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{81}$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{81}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}={3}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{81}\\right)={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-4})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=3$ and $x=-4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-4})=-4\n$$" "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{81}$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{81}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}={3}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{81}\\right)={\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{-4})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=3$ and $x=-4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{-4})=-4\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518161 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|7-(-1)|,|6-2|,|-2-4|) \\\\  & =max(8,4,6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=\\max (|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|7-(-1)|,|6-2|,|-2-4|) \\\\  & =\\max (8,4,6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518162 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|0-0.5|,|-6-(-7)|) \\\\  & =max(0.5,1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=\\max (|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|0-0.5|,|-6-(-7)|) \\\\  & =\\max (0.5,1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518163 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|-3-2|,|5-7|,|1-4|) \\\\  & =max(5,2,3) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=\\max (|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|-3-2|,|5-7|,|1-4|) \\\\  & =\\max (5,2,3) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518176 :question/problem "${log}_{4}⁡64=$" "${\\log }_{4}⁡64=$"] [:db/cas 17592186518176 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $64$ as a power of $4$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n64={4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡64={log}_{4}⁡({4}^{3})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=4$ and $x=3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{3})=3\n$$" "First, we rewrite $64$ as a power of $4$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n64={4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{4}⁡64={\\log }_{4}⁡({4}^{3})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=4$ and $x=3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{4}⁡({4}^{3})=3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518181 :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{7}⁡({7}^{2/5})$ without using a calculator." "Calculate ${\\log }_{7}⁡({7}^{2/5})$ without using a calculator."] [:db/cas 17592186518181 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=7$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{7}⁡({7}^{2/5})=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=7$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{7}⁡({7}^{2/5})=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518190 :question/problem "${log}_{10}⁡({10}^{4})=$" "${\\log }_{10}⁡({10}^{4})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186518190 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=10$ and $x=4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{10}⁡({10}^{4})=4.\n$$" "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=10$ and $x=4.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{10}⁡({10}^{4})=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518191 :question/problem "${log}_{2}⁡(256)=$" "${\\log }_{2}⁡(256)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186518191 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $256$ as a power of $2$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n256={2}^{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡256={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{8})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=2$ and $x=8.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{8})=8\n$$" "First, we rewrite $256$ as a power of $2$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n256={2}^{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{2}⁡256={\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{8})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=2$ and $x=8.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{8})=8\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518193 :question/problem "${log}_{49}⁡1=$" "${\\log }_{49}⁡1=$"] [:db/cas 17592186518193 :question/worked-solution "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=49.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{49}⁡1=0\n$$" "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡1=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=49.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{49}⁡1=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518204 :question/problem "${log}_{10}⁡(10)=$" "${\\log }_{10}⁡(10)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186518204 :question/worked-solution "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡n=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=10.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{10}⁡10=1\n$$" "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡n=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=10.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{10}⁡10=1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518208 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|5-9|,|3-(-9)|) \\\\  & =max(4,12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=\\max (|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|5-9|,|3-(-9)|) \\\\  & =\\max (4,12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518211 :question/problem "${log}_{8}⁡({8}^{7})=$" "${\\log }_{8}⁡({8}^{7})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186518211 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=8$ and $x=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{log}_{8}⁡({8}^{7})=7.\n$$" "The logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=8$ and $x=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{8}⁡({8}^{7})=7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186518217 :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{72}⁡(72).$" "Calculate ${\\log }_{72}⁡(72).$"] [:db/cas 17592186518217 :question/worked-solution "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡n=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=72.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{72}⁡72=1\n$$" "We have the general rule that:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡n=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, $n=72.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{72}⁡72=1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186519368 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{arcosh}^{2}⁡x+C" "\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{arcosh} }^{2}⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186519522 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =3 \\\\ f(0) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-8 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{3,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =3 \\\\ f(0) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-8 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{3,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186519523 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(-2) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(4+(-2)) \\\\  & =-3-2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3,-1,-5\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$" "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(-2) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(4+(-2)) \\\\  & =-3-2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=\\max \\{-3,-1,-5\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186519558 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $q(x)$ on $[1,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the 