$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186519573 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/c3/c30d7e1d2c9eb939726d85b0ee5d51a2bdc0cfe73fab92f0ba29839a9f18be4e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-7.5 \\\\  & =-5.5 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-3+7.5) \\\\  & =2-4.5 \\\\  & =-2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,-5.5,-2.5\\}=-5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$" "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/c3/c30d7e1d2c9eb939726d85b0ee5d51a2bdc0cfe73fab92f0ba29839a9f18be4e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-7.5 \\\\  & =-5.5 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-3+7.5) \\\\  & =2-4.5 \\\\  & =-2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{2,-5.5,-2.5\\}=-5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186519577 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $s(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${s}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${s}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $s$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(9) & =-2 \\\\ s(0) & =s(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{0}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =s(9)-{\\int }_{0}^{9}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2-(-6) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $s$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $s$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{s}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,4\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $s$ is ${s}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $s(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$" "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $s(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${s}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${s}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $s$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(9) & =-2 \\\\ s(0) & =s(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{0}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =s(9)-{\\int }_{0}^{9}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2-(-6) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $s$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $s$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{s}_{\\text{max}}=\\max \\{-2,4\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $s$ is ${s}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $s(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186519593 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $a(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${a}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${a}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $a$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(5) & =-4 \\\\ a(-2) & =a(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =a(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4-(-11) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $a$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $a$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{7,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $a$ is ${a}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $a(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $a(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${a}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${a}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $a$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(5) & =-4 \\\\ a(-2) & =a(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =a(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4-(-11) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $a$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $a$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{7,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $a$ is ${a}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $a(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186519595 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $r(x)$ on $[-1,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${r}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,7),$ and ${r}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $r$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(7) & =6 \\\\ r(-1) & =r(7)+{\\int }_{7}^{-1}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =r(7)-{\\int }_{-1}^{7}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6-(-10) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $r$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $r$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{6,16\\}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $r$ is ${r}_{\\text{max}}=16,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $r(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$" "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $r(x)$ on $[-1,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${r}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,7),$ and ${r}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $r$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(7) & =6 \\\\ r(-1) & =r(7)+{\\int }_{7}^{-1}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =r(7)-{\\int }_{-1}^{7}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6-(-10) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $r$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $r$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{\\text{max}}=\\max \\{6,16\\}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $r$ is ${r}_{\\text{max}}=16,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $r(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186520472 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=det({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=\\det ({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186520479 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}}]\\cdot {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}}]\\cdot {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186520480 :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "If $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({\\sin }^{2}⁡x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186520480 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡x)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{|{sin}^{2}⁡x|\\sqrt{1+{({sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{2}}}]\\cdot {({sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{4}⁡x}}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{4}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{4}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({\\sin }^{2}⁡x)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{|{\\sin }^{2}⁡x|\\sqrt{1+{({\\sin }^{2}⁡x)}^{2}}}]\\cdot {({\\sin }^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{\\sin }^{4}⁡x}}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{\\sin }^{4}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{\\sin }^{4}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186520502 :question/worked-solution "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{9-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\ln \\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{9-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186520514 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & -\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,1)=det({f}^{″}(-1,1))=(-2)^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)=({f}^{″}(-1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(-1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0,$ the point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & -\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,1)=\\det ({f}^{″}(-1,1))=(-2)^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)=({f}^{″}(-1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(-1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0,$ the point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186521455 :question/problem "Consider two sets of $40$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{40},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{40}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=54,\\,{S}_{yy}=35,\\,{S}_{xy}=188.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." "Consider two sets of $40$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{40},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{40}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=54,\\,{S}_{yy}=35,\\,{S}_{xy}=188.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186521455 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=188.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{188}{40} \\\\  & =4.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=188.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{188}{40} \\\\  & =4.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186521460 :question/problem "Consider two sets of $15$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{15},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{15}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=105,\\,{S}_{yy}=81,\\,{S}_{xy}=69.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." "Consider two sets of $15$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{15},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{15}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=105,\\,{S}_{yy}=81,\\,{S}_{xy}=69.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186521460 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=69.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{69}{15} \\\\  & =4.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=69.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{69}{15} \\\\  & =4.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186521475 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+x\\,j+3y\\,k \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t-12{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2{cos}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-7(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-5+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-5t+\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-5(2\\pi )+0)-(1-5(0)+0) \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+x\\,j+3y\\,k \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-12{\\sin }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡t-14{\\sin }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-7(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-5+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-5t+\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-5(2\\pi )+0)-(1-5(0)+0) \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186521486 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\theta $ and $\\alpha $ we use $arccos$ and $arctan$ respectively. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0.64 \\\\ \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64) \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha ) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta +\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\  & =(50.208\\,1…)^{∘}+(51.340\\,1…)^{∘} \\\\  & =(101.548\\,2…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{101.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "To find $\\theta $ and $\\alpha $ we use $\\arccos $ and $\\arctan $ respectively. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0.64 \\\\ \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64) \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha ) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta +\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\  & =(50.208\\,1…)^{∘}+(51.340\\,1…)^{∘} \\\\  & =(101.548\\,2…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{101.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186521553 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nI. $Q{Q}^{T}=I$\n\nII. ${Q}^{2}$ is the zero matrix\n\nIII. If $A=QB{Q}^{-1}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nI. $Q{Q}^{T}=I$\n\nII. ${Q}^{2}$ is the zero matrix\n\nIII. If $A=QB{Q}^{-1}$ then $|\\det (A)|=|\\det (B)|$"] [:db/cas 17592186521553 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\nIt's true by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the identity matrix\n\n$$\nQ=I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that $Q$ is an orthogonal matrix and also ${Q}^{2}=Q\\ne O.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(QB{Q}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(QB{Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(Q)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\nIt's true by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the identity matrix\n\n$$\nQ=I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that $Q$ is an orthogonal matrix and also ${Q}^{2}=Q\\ne O.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing $|\\det (A)|,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\det (A)| & =|\\det (QB{Q}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|\\det (QB{Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =|\\det (Q)|\\cdot |\\det (B)|\\cdot |\\det ({Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\det (B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|\\det (B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186522725 :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{11}⁡(7)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." "Use a calculator to evaluate ${\\log }_{11}⁡(7)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186522725 :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{11}⁡(7)$ by changing the base $11$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=11,$ $X=7,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{11}⁡(7) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(7)}{{log}_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.845\\,098}{1.041\\,393} \\\\  & ≈0.812\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's calculate ${\\log }_{11}⁡(7)$ by changing the base $11$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=11,$ $X=7,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{11}⁡(7) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡(7)}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.845\\,098}{1.041\\,393} \\\\  & ≈0.812\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186522741 :question/problem "If ${log}_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{k},$ where $k$ is an integer, then $k=${{field-1}}." "If ${\\log }_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{\\log }_{5}⁡x}{k},$ where $k$ is an integer, then $k=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186522741 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=125$ to base $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{125}⁡x & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡125} & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡({5}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, $k=3.$" "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=125$ to base $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{125}⁡x & =\\frac{{\\log }_{5}⁡x}{{\\log }_{5}⁡125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\log }_{5}⁡x}{{\\log }_{5}⁡125} & =\\frac{{\\log }_{5}⁡x}{{\\log }_{5}⁡({5}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{5}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{\\log }_{5}⁡x}{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, $k=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186522747 :question/problem "Let $\\operatorname{log}⁡x=t.$ Express ${log}_{1000}⁡x$ via the variable $t.$\n\n${log}_{1000}⁡x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Let $\\operatorname{log}⁡x=t.$ Express ${\\log }_{1000}⁡x$ via the variable $t.$\n\n${\\log }_{1000}⁡x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186522747 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=1000$ to base $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{1000}⁡x & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡(1000)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡(1000)} & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{{log}_{10}⁡({10}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{log}⁡x=t,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{1000}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡x}{3}=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$" "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=1000$ to base $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{1000}⁡x & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡x}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(1000)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡x}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(1000)} & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡x}{{\\log }_{10}⁡({10}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{log}⁡x=t,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{1000}⁡x=\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡x}{3}=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186522812 :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{8}⁡(19)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n${log}_{8}⁡(19)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "Use a calculator to evaluate ${\\log }_{8}⁡(19)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n${\\log }_{8}⁡(19)≈$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186522812 :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{8}⁡(19)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=19,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡(19) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(19)}{{log}_{10}⁡(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(19)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1.278\\,754}{0.903\\,090} \\\\  & ≈1.416\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's calculate ${\\log }_{8}⁡(19)$ by changing the base $8$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ $X=19,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{8}⁡(19) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡(19)}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(19)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1.278\\,754}{0.903\\,090} \\\\  & ≈1.416\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186523262 :answer/value "\\frac{{csch}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" "\\frac{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}"] [:db/cas 17592186523271 :answer/value "4x{csch}^{2}⁡4x" "4x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡4x"] [:db/cas 17592186523346 :answer/value "-\\frac{{csch}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" "-\\frac{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}"] [:db/cas 17592186523406 :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)" "{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)"] [:db/cas 17592186523419 :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x{tanh}^{2}⁡x)" "{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x(1-2x{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)"] [:db/cas 17592186523436 :answer/value "\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}" "\\frac{2x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186523475 :answer/value "-\\frac{2x{sech}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{{x}^{2}}" "-\\frac{2x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{{x}^{2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186523490 :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" "\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}"] [:db/cas 17592186523524 :answer/value "-6{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" "-6{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186523541 :answer/value "-\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" "-\\frac{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}"] [:db/cas 17592186523559 :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{18}\\,\\text{rad/s}" "\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{18}\\,\\text{rad/s}"] [:db/cas 17592186523575 :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" "\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}"] [:db/cas 17592186523581 :answer/value "\\frac{x{sech}^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4{x}^{2}}" "\\frac{x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4{x}^{2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186523588 :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1+2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)" "{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x(1+2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)"] [:db/cas 17592186523624 :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" "\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}"] [:db/cas 17592186523626 :answer/value "8{csch}^{2}⁡4x" "8{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡4x"] [:db/cas 17592186523632 :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x" "-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{\\coth }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186523637 :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" "\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}"] [:db/cas 17592186523648 :answer/value "-\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}" "-\\frac{2x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186523657 :answer/value "6\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x" "6\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{\\coth }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186523679 :answer/value "-8{sech}^{2}⁡4x" "-8{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡4x"] [:db/cas 17592186523687 :answer/value "3{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" "3{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186523728 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(1-2x{coth}^{2}⁡x)" "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(1-2x{\\coth }^{2}⁡x)"] [:db/cas 17592186523731 :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{sech}^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4x}" "\\frac{{x}^{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4x}"] [:db/cas 17592186523925 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x$ and $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}^{′}\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot {(2x)}^{′}}{{(2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2{csch}^{2}⁡2x)\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot (2)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x$ and $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}^{′}\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot {(2x)}^{′}}{{(2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡2x)\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot (2)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(2x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186523931 :question/worked-solution "![](images/81/813a4a253bdac3c44fe66da2728ad01bf3f1db2e0612093ce8f6a81b60e50161.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the hot air balloon, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the balloon and the observer. The quantity $y=300\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the hot air balloon stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{300}{x} \\\\ x & =300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.05$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300(2)^{2}(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hot air balloon is $60\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "![](images/81/813a4a253bdac3c44fe66da2728ad01bf3f1db2e0612093ce8f6a81b60e50161.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the hot air balloon, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the balloon and the observer. The quantity $y=300\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the hot air balloon stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{300}{x} \\\\ x & =300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.05$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300(2)^{2}(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hot air balloon is $60\\,\\text{m/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186523940 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=x{sech}^{2}⁡x.$" "Find the derivative of $f(x)=x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186523940 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={sech}^{2}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(x)}^{′}\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot {({sech}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x-2x{sech}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(x)}^{′}\\cdot {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot {({\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x-2x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186523956 :question/worked-solution "![](images/80/80b92831e73b31464fad3adeb1b2e3d9eed0a0df29e8f30b21bddd5f45bde230.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the drone and Angela. The quantity $y=5\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the altitude of the drone is constant.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{x} \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-5(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is *decreasing* at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second." "![](images/80/80b92831e73b31464fad3adeb1b2e3d9eed0a0df29e8f30b21bddd5f45bde230.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the drone and Angela. The quantity $y=5\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the altitude of the drone is constant.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{x} \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-5{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-5(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is *decreasing* at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second."] [:db/cas 17592186523958 :question/worked-solution "![](images/bd/bd5afbe33a3a82ec439c5807aa99d1d28639ed74126c2dd483093ca511b253c9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the height of the lighthouse above the water, and $x$ is the horizontal distance from the lighthouse base to the light spot on the water. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the light’s height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.12.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(0.12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the light spot toward the lighthouse is $1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{m/s}.$" "![](images/bd/bd5afbe33a3a82ec439c5807aa99d1d28639ed74126c2dd483093ca511b253c9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the height of the lighthouse above the water, and $x$ is the horizontal distance from the lighthouse base to the light spot on the water. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the light’s height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.12.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta )(0.12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.2{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the light spot toward the lighthouse is $1.2{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{m/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186523963 :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/430d9bccfa7d9063ef3be511b894f23227fc248eb6afde9a25dc78546dde32aa.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between Greta and the kite. The quantity $y=150$ inches does not change with time because the kite height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{150}{x} \\\\ x & =150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-10.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing is $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}$ radians per second." "![](images/43/430d9bccfa7d9063ef3be511b894f23227fc248eb6afde9a25dc78546dde32aa.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between Greta and the kite. The quantity $y=150$ inches does not change with time because the kite height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{150}{x} \\\\ x & =150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-150{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-10.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(-150{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =({\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing is $\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}$ radians per second."] [:db/cas 17592186523976 :question/worked-solution "![](images/d3/d30d2927efb6e85c408ba113499e043f72d726267e114e853690ac788c3946b1.png)\n\n \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the ranger and the drone's takeoff position, and $y$ is the altitude of the drone. The quantity $x=900\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between the ranger and the takeoff position is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{900} \\\\ y & =900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{60}\n$$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡({45}^{∘})\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{60}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{60}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =30\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is increasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,\\text{ft/s}.$" "![](images/d3/d30d2927efb6e85c408ba113499e043f72d726267e114e853690ac788c3946b1.png)\n\n \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the ranger and the drone's takeoff position, and $y$ is the altitude of the drone. The quantity $x=900\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between the ranger and the takeoff position is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{900} \\\\ y & =900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{60}\n$$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{\\sec }^{2}⁡({45}^{∘})\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{60}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{60}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =30\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is increasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,\\text{ft/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186523987 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x)=3{csch}^{2}⁡x.$" "Find the derivative of $f(x)=3{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186523987 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=3{g}^{2},\\,g=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6g(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=3{g}^{2},\\,g=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6g(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186524008 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡g,\\,g=4x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2{csch}^{2}\\,g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2{csch}^{2}⁡g\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8{csch}^{2}⁡g \\\\  & =8{csch}^{2}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡g,\\,g=4x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2{csch}^{2}\\,g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡g\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡g \\\\  & =8{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186524028 :question/worked-solution "![](images/f2/f28ca3385d2179984c29d27a06926806e445c1dbab2cfa5f9fd6857149b632bb.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the car and the building, and $y$ is the building's height. The quantity $y=108$ meters does not change with time because the building's height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{108} \\\\ x & =108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{36}\n$$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-108{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\pi \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}.$" "![](images/f2/f28ca3385d2179984c29d27a06926806e445c1dbab2cfa5f9fd6857149b632bb.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the car and the building, and $y$ is the building's height. The quantity $y=108$ meters does not change with time because the building's height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{108} \\\\ x & =108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{36}\n$$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-108{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\pi \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186524031 :question/worked-solution "![](images/b4/b4b48ffa8202e15042190b98b8fc11bb02bc06547abcfbfba87b1433e5161d9b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance between the lifeguard and the point on the beach directly beneath the balloon, and $y$ is the balloon's height. The quantity $x=10\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the lifeguard does not move.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =10{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{40}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$" "![](images/b4/b4b48ffa8202e15042190b98b8fc11bb02bc06547abcfbfba87b1433e5161d9b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance between the lifeguard and the point on the beach directly beneath the balloon, and $y$ is the balloon's height. The quantity $x=10\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the lifeguard does not move.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =10{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{40}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186524380 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}}"] [:db/cas 17592186524385 :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" "{\\sec }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186524467 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}" "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x}"] [:db/cas 17592186524538 :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}" "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x}"] [:db/cas 17592186524546 :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡x" "{\\tan }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186524552 :answer/value "-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}" "-\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x}"] [:db/cas 17592186524633 :answer/value "-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}" "-\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}"] [:db/cas 17592186524758 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}}"] [:db/cas 17592186524782 :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}" "-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}}"] [:db/cas 17592186524974 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186524989 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =10 \\\\ f(1) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-9 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-(-3+9) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{10,1,4\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =10 \\\\ f(1) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-9 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-(-3+9) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{10,1,4\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186524997 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=2x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((4)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\,\\sqrt{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={({4}^{2x+1})|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{2(-1)+1} \\\\  & ={4}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=2x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((4)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\ln \\,(\\,\\sqrt{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={({4}^{2x+1})|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{2(-1)+1} \\\\  & ={4}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186524999 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $det(A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $\\det (A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186524999 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-7$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-7<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-7$ and \n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-7<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186525000 :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)},$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\frac{1}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}$" "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)},$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡(2x-1)}$"] [:db/cas 17592186525000 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1).$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-(2x-1)^{2}}\\cdot (2x-1)^{′}=\\frac{2}{4x-4{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(4x-4{x}^{2})\\,{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4x-4{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{1}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1).$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡(2x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1))^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-(2x-1)^{2}}\\cdot (2x-1)^{′}=\\frac{2}{4x-4{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{(4x-4{x}^{2})\\,{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡(2x-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2x-1)}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4x-4{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186525002 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1,3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =2 \\\\ h(5) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(3)-{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2-(-6) \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(-3) & =h(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =8-(-2) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,8,10\\}=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$" "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1,3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =2 \\\\ h(5) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(3)-{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2-(-6) \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(-3) & =h(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =8-(-2) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=\\max \\{2,6,8,10\\}=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186525007 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}\\right)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{ln}^{2}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}}\\right)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186525011 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,0,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-2 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(0)-{\\int }_{-5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-11) \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,9,0,-2\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the values of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ are ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0,4.$" "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,0,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-2 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(0)-{\\int }_{-5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-11) \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=\\min \\{-2,9,0,-2\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the values of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ are ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0,4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186525015 :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{arcoth}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}+\\underbrace{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}\\right)}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){arcoth}^{2}⁡x}.$" "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)^{′}}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x}+\\underbrace{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x}\\right)}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(1-{x}^{2}){\\operatorname{arcoth} }^{2}⁡x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186525030 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(1) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-(5-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,-2,0\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$" "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(1) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-(5-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=\\max \\{3,-2,0\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186525031 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates 