ax}}=\\max \\{-4,-13\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $p$ is ${p}_{\\text{max}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $p(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186525061 :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2,0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & =3 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =3+4 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ g(3) & =g(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =7+(-7) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ g(-5) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =g(-2)-{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,7,0,-2\\}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=7,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$" "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2,0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & =3 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  