\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1]\\text{d}x & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+1]-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{1}{2}x+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186525997 :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y={log}_{2}⁡(2x+3).$" "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y={\\log }_{2}⁡(2x+3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186525997 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{2}⁡(u),\\,u=2x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={\\log }_{2}⁡(u),\\,u=2x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186525998 :question/problem "$4{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)-12{log}_{6}⁡(x)=$" "$4{\\log }_{6}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{6}⁡(2x)-12{\\log }_{6}⁡(x)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186525998 :question/worked-solution "Both the first and the third logarithmic terms have a base of $6$ and an argument of $x.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)-12{log}_{6}⁡(x) & =(4-12){log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-8{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Both the first and the third logarithmic terms have a base of $6$ and an argument of $x.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\log }_{6}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{6}⁡(2x)-12{\\log }_{6}⁡(x) & =(4-12){\\log }_{6}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{6}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-8{\\log }_{6}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{6}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526017 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\{arcsch[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\{\\operatorname{arcsch} [\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526019 :question/problem "If $4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x=1,$ then what is $x?$" "If $4{\\log }_{9}⁡x-2{\\log }_{9}⁡x=1,$ then what is $x?$"] [:db/cas 17592186526019 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (4-2){log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $9,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x} & ={9}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={9}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\log }_{9}⁡x-2{\\log }_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (4-2){\\log }_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 2{\\log }_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ {\\log }_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $9,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {9}^{{\\log }_{9}⁡x} & ={9}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${9}^{{\\log }_{9}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={9}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526024 :question/problem "If ${log}_{36}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2},$ then what is $x?$" "If ${\\log }_{36}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2},$ then what is $x?$"] [:db/cas 17592186526024 :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $36,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x} & ={36}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={36}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $36,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{36}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {36}^{{\\log }_{36}⁡x} & ={36}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${36}^{{\\log }_{36}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={36}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526032 :question/problem "If $\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3}=2$, then $x=$" "If $\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3}=2$, then $x=$"] [:db/cas 17592186526032 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3} & =2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)} & ={3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}=x+24.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+24 & ={3}^{6} \\\\ x+24 & =729 \\\\ x & =705.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3} & =2 \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6 \\\\ {3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+24)} & ={3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+24)}=x+24.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+24 & ={3}^{6} \\\\ x+24 & =729 \\\\ x & =705.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526033 :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y({sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}({sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x.$" "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y({\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}({\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186526034 :question/problem "![](images/c9/c91e196377aad64db49224cb5e38c1c8336171649708ec78b9cb80ebe6803128.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/c9/c91e196377aad64db49224cb5e38c1c8336171649708ec78b9cb80ebe6803128.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186526034 :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite the above formula as\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x)\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{3}}{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )+\\frac{(-\\pi /2)^{3}}{3}+3\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{24}+\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-(-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{24}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{12}+3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite the above formula as\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x)\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{3}}{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )+\\frac{(-\\pi /2)^{3}}{3}+3\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{24}+\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-(-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{24}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{12}+3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526048 :question/worked-solution "The area of the region bounded by the curve $y=6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))={sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{(x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right))-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))] \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-1)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)] \\\\  & =3(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the region bounded by the curve $y=6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))={sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{(x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right))-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))] \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-1)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)] \\\\  & =3(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526054 :question/problem "If ${log}_{7}⁡(a-3)=4$, then $a=$" "If ${\\log }_{7}⁡(a-3)=4$, then $a=$"] [:db/cas 17592186526054 :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(a-3) & =4 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(a-3)} & ={7}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(a-3)}=a-3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-3 & ={7}^{4} \\\\ a-3 & =2401 \\\\ a & =2404.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{7}⁡(a-3) & =4 \\\\ {7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(a-3)} & ={7}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(a-3)}=a-3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-3 & ={7}^{4} \\\\ a-3 & =2401 \\\\ a & =2404.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526067 :question/problem "Given the function $y=-3{log}_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." "Given the function $y=-3{\\log }_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186526067 :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{log}_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{\\log }_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{2}⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526082 :question/problem "If $3{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)=6$, then $x=$" "If $3{\\log }_{5}⁡(x+2)=6$, then $x=$"] [:db/cas 17592186526082 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)}=x+2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =25 \\\\ x & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\log }_{5}⁡(x+2) & =6 \\\\ {\\log }_{5}⁡(x+2) & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ {\\log }_{5}⁡(x+3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡(x+2) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{\\log }_{5}⁡(x+2)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{\\log }_{5}⁡(x+2)}=x+2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =25 \\\\ x & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526083 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={3}^{x}-5{log}_{2}⁡x.$" "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={3}^{x}-5{\\log }_{2}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186526083 :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}-5{log}_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{5}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}-5{\\log }_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{5}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526096 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $7{log}_{2}⁡3-{log}_{2}⁡3?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to $7{\\log }_{2}⁡3-{\\log }_{2}⁡3?$"] [:db/cas 17592186526096 :question/worked-solution "Both logarithmic terms have a base of $2$ and an argument of $3.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{log}_{2}⁡3-{log}_{2}⁡3 & =(7-1){log}_{2}⁡3=6{log}_{2}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Both logarithmic terms have a base of $2$ and an argument of $3.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\log }_{2}⁡3-{\\log }_{2}⁡3 & =(7-1){\\log }_{2}⁡3=6{\\log }_{2}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526104 :question/problem "If ${log}_{5}⁡(a-10)=3$, then $a=$" "If ${\\log }_{5}⁡(a-10)=3$, then $a=$"] [:db/cas 17592186526104 :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(a-10) & =3 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)} & ={5}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)}=a-10.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-10 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ a-10 & =125 \\\\ a & =135.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡(a-10) & =3 \\\\ {5}^{{\\log }_{5}⁡(a-10)} & ={5}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{\\log }_{5}⁡(a-10)}=a-10.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-10 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ a-10 & =125 \\\\ a & =135.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186526497 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})" "{\\log }_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})"] [:db/cas 17592186526512 :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186526636 :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186526641 :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{2})" "1-{\\log }_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{2})"] [:db/cas 17592186526753 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{3})" "{\\log }_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{3})"] [:db/cas 17592186526821 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+y)" "{\\log }_{2}⁡(1+y)"] [:db/cas 17592186526869 :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186526905 :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186526936 :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{3})" "1-{\\log }_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{3})"] [:db/cas 17592186527148 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}-2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=2{y}^{2}-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2})=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2})=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2}-2 & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=0\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-16.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=\\det ({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}-2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=2{y}^{2}-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2})=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2})=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2}-2 & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=0\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-16.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186527157 :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={log}_{2}⁡(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={\\log }_{2}⁡(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186527157 :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+(1){y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(1+(1){y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186527172 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={\\sin }^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?"] [:db/cas 17592186527172 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\sin }^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t(1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\sqrt{1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186527930 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $sec$  is the reciprocal of $cos,$  $csc$ is the reciprocal of $sin$  and $cot$  is the reciprocal of $tan.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}}.\n$$\n\nThe angle ${60}^{∘}$ and ${30}^{∘}$ are special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘} &= 6\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}\\right)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}+\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)} \\\\ &= 12+6+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that $\\sec $  is the reciprocal of $cos,$  $\\csc $ is the reciprocal of $\\sin $  and $\\cot $  is the reciprocal of $tan.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}}.\n$$\n\nThe angle ${60}^{∘}$ and ${30}^{∘}$ are special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘} &= 6\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}\\right)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}+\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)} \\\\ &= 12+6+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &= 18+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186527959 :question/problem "$5{cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" "$5{\\cos }^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{\\tan }^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186527959 :question/worked-solution "The angle ${45}^{∘}$ is special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that ${sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2},$ ${cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}$ and  ${tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘} &= 5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}+3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}-7(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2} \\\\ &= 5{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-7(1)^{2} \\\\ &= 8{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-7 \\\\ &= 8\\left(\\frac{2}{4}\\right)-7 \\\\ &= 4-7 \\\\ &= -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The angle ${45}^{∘}$ is special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that ${\\sin }^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2},$ ${\\cos }^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}$ and  ${\\tan }^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\cos }^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{\\tan }^{2}⁡{45}^{∘} &= 5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}+3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}-7(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2} \\\\ &= 5{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-7(1)^{2} \\\\ &= 8{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-7 \\\\ &= 8\\left(\\frac{2}{4}\\right)-7 \\\\ &= 4-7 \\\\ &= -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186527994 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186527994 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =3x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2) & ={sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2) \\\\ {tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2) & =1-{sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1-{sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =3x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡(3x-2) & ={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡(3x-2) \\\\ {\\tanh }^{2}⁡(3x-2) & =1-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡(3x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡(3x-2))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡(3x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186528407 :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186528409 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186528452 :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186528528 :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186528551 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186528728 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186528738 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186528833 :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186528842 :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186529107 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{9}{2}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9}{2}\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}{8}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+4) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{9\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{9}{2}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9}{2}\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}{8}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+4) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{9\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529112 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=2\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(9-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{8}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-9(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{9}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{8}-9+\\frac{9}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi -72}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=2\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(9-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{8}\\,{|}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-9(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{9}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{8}-9+\\frac{9}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi -72}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529113 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}-\\frac{\\pi }{8})+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}-\\frac{\\pi }{8})+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529122 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529133 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+5{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+\\frac{5(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}{2}+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{13}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{13}{2}\\theta -\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{13\\pi }{4}+6) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{13\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi +24}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(9{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+5{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4+\\frac{5(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}{2}+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{13}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{13}{2}\\theta -\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{13\\pi }{4}+6) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{13\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi +24}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529135 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(\\pi -0)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0+\\frac{1}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(\\pi -0)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0+\\frac{1}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529147 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\theta +\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\theta +\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529148 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =2\\pi +0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =2\\pi +0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529149 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529160 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +8(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta +24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+12(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+12(\\sqrt{3})+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+14\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(3+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +8(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta +24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(17\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+12(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+12(\\sqrt{3})+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}+14\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529179 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4)\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186529209 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}- \\sqrt{2})+1(- \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+4 \\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{20}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$Q$ is a singular matrix” and $det(Q)=0$ are true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}- \\sqrt{2})+1(- \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+4 \\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{20}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$Q$ is a singular matrix” and $\\det (Q)=0$ are true."] [:db/cas 17592186529214 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{4})+(- \\sqrt{2})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\sqrt{2})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} \\\\  & =1- \\frac{7}{10}- \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{13}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(R)=- \\frac{13}{10}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$R$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{4})+(- \\sqrt{2})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\sqrt{2})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} \\\\  & =1- \\frac{7}{10}- \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{13}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (R)=- \\frac{13}{10}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$R$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186529219 :answer-field/key "$det(D)=0$" "$\\det (D)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186529220 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\frac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{25}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{3}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{6}{25}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\frac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{25}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{3}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (D)=\\frac{6}{25}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186529229 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{8})+(-1)(- \\frac{7}{5}+\\frac{21}{20})+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}+(-1)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{20}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{40}+\\frac{7}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$ are true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{8})+(-1)(- \\frac{7}{5}+\\frac{21}{20})+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{10}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}+(-1)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{20}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{40}+\\frac{7}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (D)=\\frac{7}{40}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $\\det (D)=\\frac{7}{40}$ are true."] [:db/cas 17592186529231 :answer-field/key "$det(Q)=0$" "$\\det (Q)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186529236 :answer-field/key "$det(D)=\\frac{7}{40}$" "$\\det (D)=\\frac{7}{40}$"] [:db/cas 17592186529243 :answer-field/key "$det(N)=0$" "$\\det (N)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186529248 :answer-field/key "$det(R)=\\frac{4}{5} - \\sqrt{2}$" "$\\det (R)=\\frac{4}{5} - \\sqrt{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186529250 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2})+(-1)(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+2 \\sqrt{2}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{10 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{9}- \\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$N$ is a singular matrix” and $det(N)=0$ are true." "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (N) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2})+(-1)(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+2 \\sqrt{2}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{10 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{9}- \\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$N$ is a singular matrix” and $\\det (N)=0$ are true."] [:db/cas 17592186529619 :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}+⋯" "{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}+⋯"] [:db/cas 17592186529792 :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x={x}^{2}+2{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+⋯" "{\\cos }^{2}⁡x={x}^{2}+2{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+⋯"] [:db/cas 17592186529872 :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186529953 :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x=1+{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}+⋯" "{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=1+{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}+⋯"] [:db/cas 17592186530145 :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{4}+⋯" "{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{4}+⋯"] [:db/cas 17592186530183 :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x={x}^{2}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+⋯" "{\\cos }^{2}⁡x={x}^{2}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+⋯"] [:db/cas 17592186530426 :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${cos}^{2}⁡x.$" "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${\\cos }^{2}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186530426 :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{x}^{4}-⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the expansion of ${cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we multiply the expansion by itself, as follows:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=(1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯)(1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯)\n$$\n\nLet's work out each term individually:\n\n- There is a single constant term, given by\n\n$$\n1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\n- There are two ${x}^{2}$ terms, and their sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\cdot 1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- There are three ${x}^{4}$ terms, and their sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4})\\cdot 1+(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first three nonzero terms of the expansion are\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$" "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{x}^{4}-⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the expansion of ${\\cos }^{2}⁡x,$ we multiply the expansion by itself, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=(1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯)(1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯)\n$$\n\nLet's work out each term individually:\n\n- There is a single constant term, given by\n\n$$\n1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\n- There are two ${x}^{2}$ terms, and their sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\cdot 1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- There are three ${x}^{4}$ terms, and their sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4})\\cdot 1+(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first three nonzero terms of the expansion are\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530457 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{2}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{2}^{2t}{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530493 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right))) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t/2)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{csc}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[u]}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right))) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t/2)}\\cdot {\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{csc}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{\\csc }^{2}⁡t-2+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{\\csc }^{2}⁡t-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{\\csc }^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{\\cot }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[u]}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530898 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({3}^{\\pi }-1)" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3+{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({3}^{\\pi }-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186530950 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1)" "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186530986 :answer/value "3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" "3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186531036 :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186531097 :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186531230 :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{4}⁡t{\\cos }^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186531261 :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186531346 :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1)" "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1)"] [:db/cas 17592186531565 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{3}^{2\\theta }+{3}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡3){3}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{3}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{\\pi }-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={3}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({3}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({3}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{3}^{2\\theta }+{3}^{2\\theta }{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡3){3}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{3}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {3}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot ({3}^{\\pi }-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531584 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-2.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531587 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={\\cos }^{3}⁡t,\\,y={\\sin }^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?"] [:db/cas 17592186531587 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot |3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\cos }^{3}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\sin }^{3}⁡t)=3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{\\sin }^{2}⁡t{\\cos }^{4}⁡t+9{\\sin }^{4}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{\\sin }^{2}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{\\sin }^{2}⁡t{\\cos }^{2}⁡t({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\cdot |3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531626 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531628 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}3\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +9{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}3\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186531644 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={2}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({2}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{2}^{2\\theta }+{2}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2){2}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{2}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {2}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({2}^{\\pi /2}-{2}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={2}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({2}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({2}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{2}^{2\\theta }+{2}^{2\\theta }{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡2){2}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{2}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {2}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({2}^{\\pi /2}-{2}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532123 :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡\\pi " "{\\ln }^{3}⁡\\pi "] [:db/cas 17592186532658 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]}^{2}+{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)=1$ and ${sec}^{2}⁡(t)-1={tan}^{2}⁡(t)$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{tan}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot |\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-2\\pi [\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]}^{2}+{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(t)+{\\sec }^{2}⁡(t)-2+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{\\sin }^{2}⁡(t)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${\\sin }^{2}⁡(t)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)=1$ and ${\\sec }^{2}⁡(t)-1={\\tan }^{2}⁡(t)$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{\\sin }^{2}⁡(t)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{2}⁡(t)-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{\\sec }^{2}⁡(t)-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{\\tan }^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot |\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-2\\pi [\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186532715 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}-\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}-\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533264 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(t+1){\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186533597 :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186533602 :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1){\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186533984 :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of determinants." "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $\\det (Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of determinants."] [:db/cas 17592186533984 :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by the properties of determinants." "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\n\\det (Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by the properties of determinants."] [:db/cas 17592186533992 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=(\\det (X))^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533992 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}=({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2det(X)\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=2(4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=(\\det (X))^{2}=({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\det (X)\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=2(4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533993 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\det (X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186533993 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{-4-(-3)} & \\frac{1}{-4-(-3)} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-4-(-3)} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-4-(-3)}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{-1} & \\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\det (X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{-4-(-3)} & \\frac{1}{-4-(-3)} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-4-(-3)} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-4-(-3)}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{-1} & \\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534099 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y={ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]?$" "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y={\\ln }^{2}⁡(t+1)\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186534099 :question/worked-solution "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1)(-{sec}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{ln}^{2}⁡(t+1){sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{\\sec }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(t+1)(-{\\sec }^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(t+1){\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-(-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(t+1){\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(t+1){\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534217 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $\\det (R)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534218 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $\\det (Q)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534226 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $\\det (H)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534234 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $\\det (A)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534237 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $\\det (Q)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534244 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $\\det (Q)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534246 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $\\det (Q)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534251 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}." "If $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $\\det (W)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186534597 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186534703 :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-1\\,\\text{d}t" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-1\\,\\text{d}t"] [:db/cas 17592186534970 :question/problem "If $-16{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)=-48$, then $x=$" "If $-16{\\log }_{7}⁡(x-128)=-48$, then $x=$"] [:db/cas 17592186534970 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =-48 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =\\frac{-48}{-16} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)} & ={7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)}=x-128.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-128 & ={7}^{3} \\\\ x-128 & =343 \\\\ x & =471.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{\\log }_{7}⁡(x-128) & =-48 \\\\ {\\log }_{7}⁡(x-128) & =\\frac{-48}{-16} \\\\ {\\log }_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3 \\\\ {7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x-128)} & ={7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x-128)}=x-128.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-128 & ={7}^{3} \\\\ x-128 & =343 \\\\ x & =471.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534981 :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25{cos}^{2}⁡t-25{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-25{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534983 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534985 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=(-2)\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}=-12<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=\\det ({f}^{″}(1,1))=(-2)\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}=-12<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186534996 :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535014 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =16(1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +16{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =16(1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$"] [:db/cas 17592186535023 :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+({sin}^{2}⁡(2t)-{1}^{2})\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+({\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t)-{1}^{2})\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535044 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}-2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}-2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535048 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-4 & 2(xy-2)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ xy-2=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{-2}=-1,\\,\\,y=2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-4)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-4)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,-2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(-1,-2))=0\\cdot (-2)-(-4)^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,2)=det({f}^{″}(1,2))=0\\cdot 2-{4}^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2).$" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-4 & 2(xy-2)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ xy-2=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{-2}=-1,\\,\\,y=2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-4)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-4)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,-2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,-2)=\\det ({f}^{″}(-1,-2))=0\\cdot (-2)-(-4)^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,2)=\\det ({f}^{″}(1,2))=0\\cdot 2-{4}^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186535054 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4x & e-{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x=0 \\\\ e-{e}^{y}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second  equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ne-{e}^{y}=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$   \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e-{e}^{y})=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(e-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))=(-4)\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=4e>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=-4<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,1).$" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4x & e-{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x=0 \\\\ e-{e}^{y}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second  equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ne-{e}^{y}=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$   \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e-{e}^{y})=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(e-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=\\det ({f}^{″}(0,1))=(-4)\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=4e>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=-4<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186535057 :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,k \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2t\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9-4t\\,dt \\\\  & =(9t-2{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(9-\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,k \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2t\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+9{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9-4t\\,dt \\\\  & =(9t-2{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(9-\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535330 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535342 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535415 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 8{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(8{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-16z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =16z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{curl} \\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 8{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(8{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-16z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =16z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535416 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 7{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(7{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-14z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =14z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{curl} \\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 7{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(7{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-14z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =14z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186535445 :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y"] [:db/cas 17592186535569 :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y"] [:db/cas 17592186535672 :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y"] [:db/cas 17592186535784 :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y"] [:db/cas 17592186535826 :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y"] [:db/cas 17592186535957 :question/problem "![](images/90/90980bb04eb7dd9906ec9a99ea3a1b7cfd9c00969bbd9b12c60fe0f80eb88840.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+2{y}^{2}=1$ and $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)$ for $y\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" "![](images/90/90980bb04eb7dd9906ec9a99ea3a1b7cfd9c00969bbd9b12c60fe0f80eb88840.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+2{y}^{2}=1$ and $x=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)$ for $y\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?"] [:db/cas 17592186535957 :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equation of our first curve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2{y}^{2} & =1\\,⟹\\,x=1-2{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/16/16ca1c96786eb8e7ef12608f70d03b34b679e63d34ef41135b4fd6801bcccc27.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right).$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right).$\n\n![](images/27/276dea7b2d9f50e3fe171cceb64c1c7a12f84cfb18bdbe242102802ad9790f49.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right))\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c6fb140bb3eaf4c49cba76af157fb46a568bc2dc24e4763facede62720f844.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(1-2{y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[2{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right))}^{2}-{(2{y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equation of our first curve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2{y}^{2} & =1\\,⟹\\,x=1-2{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/16/16ca1c96786eb8e7ef12608f70d03b34b679e63d34ef41135b4fd6801bcccc27.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right).$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right).$\n\n![](images/27/276dea7b2d9f50e3fe171cceb64c1c7a12f84cfb18bdbe242102802ad9790f49.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right))\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c6fb140bb3eaf4c49cba76af157fb46a568bc2dc24e4763facede62720f844.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(1-2{y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[2{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right))}^{2}-{(2{y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536301 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)={cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡y" "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)={\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡y"] [:db/cas 17592186536355 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x={sinh}^{2}⁡x+{cosh}^{2}⁡x" "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x={\\sinh }^{2}⁡x+{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186536357 :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" "-\\frac{1}{3}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{3}⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186536388 :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1" "{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=1"] [:db/cas 17592186536390 :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1" "{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=1"] [:db/cas 17592186536399 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)={sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡y" "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)={\\sinh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡y"] [:db/cas 17592186536415 :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=e" "{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=e"] [:db/cas 17592186536442 :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=-1" "{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=-1"] [:db/cas 17592186536485 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" "\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{4}⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186536521 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{csch}^{3}⁡x+C" "\\frac{1}{3}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{3}⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186536536 :answer/value "{cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x=-1" "{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=-1"] [:db/cas 17592186536600 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x={sinh}^{2}⁡x-{cosh}^{2}⁡x" "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x={\\sinh }^{2}⁡x-{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x"] [:db/cas 17592186536613 :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}{sech}^{4}⁡x+C" "-\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{4}⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186536658 :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C" "-\\frac{1}{3}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{3}⁡x+C"] [:db/cas 17592186536814 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to express the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to express the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡x(5\\operatorname{coth}⁡x-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+3\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536826 :question/problem "$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536826 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}{u}^{-3}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{32}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{288}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}{u}^{-3}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{32}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{288}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536832 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nUsing $x=\\theta $ in the above identity, the integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nUsing $x=\\theta $ in the above identity, the integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536840 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186536840 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡(0)+\\frac{1}{2}{sech}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2})^{2}}-\\frac{4}{({e}^{0}+{e}^{0})^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1/2+2)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡(0)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2})^{2}}-\\frac{4}{({e}^{0}+{e}^{0})^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1/2+2)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536849 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536849 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity and rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sech}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{cosh}^{3}⁡x}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{sech}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity and rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{\\cosh }^{3}⁡x}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536864 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}{2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186536864 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {tanh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {tanh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{sech}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}{2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\tanh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\tanh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536879 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{coth}^{2}⁡x{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\coth }^{2}⁡x{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186536879 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{coth}^{2}⁡x{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}-{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-{3}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\coth }^{2}⁡x{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}-{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-{3}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536885 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{sech}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186536885 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{sech}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2+{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{2}}{2+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2+{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{2}}{2+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536891 :question/problem "Use a calculator to evaluate ${log}_{7}⁡(12)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places." "Use a calculator to evaluate ${\\log }_{7}⁡(12)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186536891 :question/worked-solution "Let's calculate ${log}_{7}⁡(12)$ by changing the base $7$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=7,$ $X=12,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(12) & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(12)}{{log}_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(12)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1.079\\,18}{0.845\\,10} \\\\  & ≈1.28\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's calculate ${\\log }_{7}⁡(12)$ by changing the base $7$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=7,$ $X=12,$ $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{7}⁡(12) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{10}⁡(12)}{{\\log }_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(12)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1.079\\,18}{0.845\\,10} \\\\  & ≈1.28\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536894 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186536894 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\coth }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\coth }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536896 :question/problem "Calculate ${log}_{36}⁡(216)$ without using a calculator." "Calculate ${\\log }_{36}⁡(216)$ without using a calculator."] [:db/cas 17592186536896 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=36,$ and $X=216.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $6,$ we set $b=6.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡(216) & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡(216)}{{log}_{6}⁡(36)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{3})}{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{\\log }_{b}⁡X}{{\\log }_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=36,$ and $X=216.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $6,$ we set $b=6.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{36}⁡(216) & =\\frac{{\\log }_{6}⁡(216)}{{\\log }_{6}⁡(36)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\log }_{6}⁡({6}^{3})}{{\\log }_{6}⁡({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536899 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186536899 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {sinh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}u\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{25}{9}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}u\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{25}{9}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536900 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536900 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to express the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-{4}^{-1}}+\\frac{2}{2-{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to express the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-{4}^{-1}}+\\frac{2}{2-{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186536983 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537003 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ce/cee08173667c9577f4c7aab8cac5386434f2e1ecab8836150ebe8066d1cbaa68.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 5=r^{2} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ce/cee08173667c9577f4c7aab8cac5386434f2e1ecab8836150ebe8066d1cbaa68.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 5=r^{2} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537006 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/57/5764f328a614ddaa96abd28a4568c4cd7dfcf0988033d3122bdce0e1e52b7bc5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}=\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/57/5764f328a614ddaa96abd28a4568c4cd7dfcf0988033d3122bdce0e1e52b7bc5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}=\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, usin