+ 2=r^{2} + 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5e/5ed28b1e7b49276cdb59da432056597e5992b8c4015ac8e9384f6bfe3f1fb7ba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 2=r^{2} + 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537289 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})" "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})"] [:db/cas 17592186537294 :answer/value "{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" "{r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -r"] [:db/cas 17592186537329 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)-4" "{f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{7}⁡(x-2)-4"] [:db/cas 17592186537374 :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" "{r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1"] [:db/cas 17592186537377 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2" "{f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{7}⁡(x-4)+2"] [:db/cas 17592186537386 :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" "{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -1"] [:db/cas 17592186537431 :answer/value "{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" "{r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -r"] [:db/cas 17592186537587 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" "{f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})+2"] [:db/cas 17592186537589 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})" "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{\\log }_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})"] [:db/cas 17592186537612 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x-4)}{2}" "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x-4)}{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186537662 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x)}{4}+2" "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{\\log }_{7}⁡(x)}{4}+2"] [:db/cas 17592186537683 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)+4" "{f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{7}⁡(x-2)+4"] [:db/cas 17592186537684 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)" "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(4x+2)"] [:db/cas 17592186537687 :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" "{f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+2"] [:db/cas 17592186537879 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{1-{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537879 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1-(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡x)}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1-(1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537880 :question/problem "The function $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x-1}{k}\\right)$ has an inverse function given by $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x+2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$ What is the value of $k?$" "The function $f(x)={\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x-1}{k}\\right)$ has an inverse function given by $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x+2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$ What is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186537880 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x-1}{k}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5y-1}{k}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5y-1}{k}\\right) \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡((5y-1)/k)} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{5y-1}{k} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ 5y-1 & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k \\\\ 5y & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k={3}^{2}=9.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x-1}{k}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5y-1}{k}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5y-1}{k}\\right) \\\\ {3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡((5y-1)/k)} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{5y-1}{k} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ 5y-1 & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k \\\\ 5y & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k={3}^{2}=9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537881 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({cosh}^{3}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({\\cosh }^{3}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537881 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({cosh}^{3}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x({cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({\\cosh }^{3}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x({\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537882 :question/problem "The inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\n$$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." "The inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\n$$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186537882 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 4x-4 & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4x-4 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y/4-1/2)} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & ={3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=4({3}^{4x-4})+2.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 4x-4 & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4x-4 \\\\ {3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡(y/4-1/2)} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & ={3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=4({3}^{4x-4})+2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537885 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{cosh}^{2}⁡2x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡2x+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537885 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x={cosh}^{2}⁡2x+{sinh}^{2}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{cosh}^{2}⁡2x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cosh}^{2}⁡2x+{sinh}^{2}⁡4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x={\\cosh }^{2}⁡2x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡2x+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({\\cosh }^{2}⁡2x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537886 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537886 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[1-16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(9-8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(9x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =[9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)]-[9\\cdot 0-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2({2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[1-16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(9-8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(9x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =[9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)]-[9\\cdot 0-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2({2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537887 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({cosh}^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-2}}\n$)\\,+\\,$\n {{field-3}}" "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-2}}\n$)\\,+\\,$\n {{field-3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537887 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({cosh}^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537889 :question/problem "If the function $f(x)=3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{3}\\right)-k?$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+6)/3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" "If the function $f(x)=3{\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{3}\\right)-k?$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+6)/3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537889 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{3}\\right)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right)-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right)-k \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right) & =x+k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right) & =\\frac{x+k}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/3)} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y-1 & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=6.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3{\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{3}\\right)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3{\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right)-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right)-k \\\\ 3{\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right) & =x+k \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{3}\\right) & =\\frac{x+k}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡((y-1)/3)} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y-1 & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537890 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537890 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2[{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\cdot \\text{d}x+\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x+{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2[{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\cdot \\text{d}x+\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x+{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537891 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{sinh}^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{\\sinh }^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537891 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{sinh}^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{\\sinh }^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537892 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,$\n{{field-3}}" "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,$\n{{field-3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537892 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cosh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1)+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1)+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537895 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x={cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+1)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2({cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C \\\\  & \\,=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x={\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+1)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2({\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C \\\\  & \\,=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537896 :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186537896 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2y-k & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{k}{2}\n$$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {\\log }_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2y-k & ={\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2y & ={\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{2-3x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{k}{2}\n$$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537900 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537900 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x+{\\sinh }^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x(1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537902 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-1}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{\\coth }^{4}⁡x-1}{1+{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537902 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡x-1={csch}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-1}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{({coth}^{2}⁡x-1)({coth}^{2}⁡x+1)}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({coth}^{2}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{17}{15}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x={\\csc }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n{\\coth }^{2}⁡x-1={\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{\\coth }^{4}⁡x-1}{1+{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{({\\coth }^{2}⁡x-1)({\\coth }^{2}⁡x+1)}{1+{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({\\coth }^{2}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{17}{15}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537905 :question/problem "If the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{5}\\right)-k$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x-3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" "If the function $f(x)={\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{5}\\right)-k$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x-3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537905 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{5}\\right)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{5}\\right)-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{5}\\right)-k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{5}\\right) & =x+k \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/5)} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{5} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ y-1 & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k} \\\\ y & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-1}{5}\\right)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{5}\\right)-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{5}\\right)-k \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y-1}{5}\\right) & =x+k \\\\ {2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡((y-1)/5)} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{5} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ y-1 & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k} \\\\ y & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537909 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)-\\,$\n{{field-3}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)-\\,$\n{{field-3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537909 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{sinh}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537912 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}{1-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537912 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{sech}^{2}⁡x={tanh}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{3}^{2}+1}{{3}^{2}-1}+\\frac{{2}^{2}+1}{{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-1={\\tan }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x={\\tanh }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}{1-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{3}^{2}+1}{{3}^{2}-1}+\\frac{{2}^{2}+1}{{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537915 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537915 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x) & =1 \\\\ 1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x) & ={cosh}^{2}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x))}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(2x)-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x) & =1 \\\\ 1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x) & ={\\cosh }^{2}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2(1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x))}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537921 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3} \\\\ 3x & ={8}^{6y} \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{8}⁡({8}^{6y}) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & =6y \\\\ 6y & ={log}_{8}⁡(3x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3} \\\\ 3x & ={8}^{6y} \\\\ {\\log }_{8}⁡(3x) & ={\\log }_{8}⁡({8}^{6y}) \\\\ {\\log }_{8}⁡(3x) & =6y \\\\ 6y & ={\\log }_{8}⁡(3x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{\\log }_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{\\log }_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537923 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{cosh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,-\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,-\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186537923 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{cosh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2})]-[0-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2})]-[0-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537925 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537925 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537926 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537926 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537928 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,{coth}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{\\coth }^{4}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537928 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,{coth}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{|}_{2}^{7} \\\\  & =7-2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{\\csc }^{2}⁡x-1={\\cot }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n{\\coth }^{2}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{\\coth }^{4}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x({\\coth }^{2}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)}{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}({\\coth }^{2}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{|}_{2}^{7} \\\\  & =7-2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537929 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537929 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot (1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡0) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-(0)}}{2}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot (1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡0) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-(0)}}{2}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537930 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537930 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =2x-1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1) & =1 \\\\ {cosh}^{2}⁡(2x-1) & =1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =2x-1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(2x-1)-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x-1) & =1 \\\\ {\\cosh }^{2}⁡(2x-1) & =1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537934 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2{sinh}^{2}⁡3x+2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡3x+2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537934 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x={cosh}^{2}⁡3x+{sinh}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{sinh}^{2}⁡3x+2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2({cosh}^{2}⁡3x+{sinh}^{2}⁡3x)-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x-x){|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x={\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡3x+2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2({\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡3x)-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x-x){|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537943 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}{1-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537943 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{sech}^{2}⁡x={tanh}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{sech}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2-{2}^{-1}}-\\frac{4+{4}^{-1}}{4-{4}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{17}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n{\\sec }^{2}⁡x-1={\\tan }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x={\\tanh }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}{1-{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2-{2}^{-1}}-\\frac{4+{4}^{-1}}{4-{4}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{17}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537944 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+{csch}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+{\\operatorname{csch} }^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537944 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1+{csch}^{2}⁡x={coth}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+{csch}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(1+{csch}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-{2}^{-1}}-\\frac{2}{3-{3}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{\\csc }^{2}⁡x-1={\\cot }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1+{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x={\\coth }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+{\\operatorname{csch} }^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(1+{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot {\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-{2}^{-1}}-\\frac{2}{3-{3}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537946 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={7}^{x-2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4 \\\\ x-4 & ={7}^{y-2} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & ={log}_{7}⁡({7}^{y-2}) \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & =y-2 \\\\ y & ={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={7}^{x-2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4 \\\\ x-4 & ={7}^{y-2} \\\\ {\\log }_{7}⁡(x-4) & ={\\log }_{7}⁡({7}^{y-2}) \\\\ {\\log }_{7}⁡(x-4) & =y-2 \\\\ y & ={\\log }_{7}⁡(x-4)+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{7}⁡(x-4)+2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537949 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{2-2{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{2-2{\\tanh }^{4}⁡x}{1+{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537949 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{2-2{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1-{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}-2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{0}-1}{{e}^{0}+1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2}-1}{{2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{2-2{\\tanh }^{4}⁡x}{1+{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1-{\\tanh }^{4}⁡x}{1+{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{(1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)(1+{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)}{1+{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}-2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{0}-1}{{e}^{0}+1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2}-1}{{2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537955 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0c/0cb340ef33d4cc38081e3796b4c8e076f1499760c0a3ffeecb8123a41fefeec8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0c/0cb340ef33d4cc38081e3796b4c8e076f1499760c0a3ffeecb8123a41fefeec8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537963 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537963 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n2{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{45}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{45}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537964 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8.$" "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+8.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537964 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equations, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(y) & =x-8 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y)} & ={2}^{x-8} \\\\ y & ={2}^{x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(y)+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equations, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(y)+8 \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(y) & =x-8 \\\\ {2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)} & ={2}^{x-8} \\\\ y & ={2}^{x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537967 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6}{1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537967 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =3x+2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2) & =1 \\\\ 1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2) & ={cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{{cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {sech}^{2}⁡(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =3x+2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(3x+2)-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(3x+2) & =1 \\\\ 1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(3x+2) & ={\\cosh }^{2}⁡(3x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{1+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537970 :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537970 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡3x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(2x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x)|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})]-[2\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(2x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x)|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})]-[2\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537971 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537971 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[2-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(8-6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(8x-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)]-[0-0] \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\left(\\frac{{3}^{2}-{3}^{-2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{40}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[2-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(8-6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(8x-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)]-[0-0] \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\left(\\frac{{3}^{2}-{3}^{-2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{40}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537975 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1+{csch}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1+{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537975 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1+{csch}^{2}⁡x={coth}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1+{csch}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-{8}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{16}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{\\csc }^{2}⁡x-1={\\cot }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1+{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x={\\coth }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1+{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-{8}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{16}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537976 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{3}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y} \\\\ -x & ={3}^{4y} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & ={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4y}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & =4y \\\\ 4y & ={log}_{3}⁡(-x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{3}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y} \\\\ -x & ={3}^{4y} \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(-x) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{4y}) \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(-x) & =4y \\\\ 4y & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(-x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537979 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y} & =x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {3}^{2y} & =4x+2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2y}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2).$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y} & =x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {3}^{2y} & =4x+2 \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{2y}) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ 2y & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(4x+2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186537985 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{csch}^{3}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{3}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537985 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={csch}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =7x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{coth}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-1 & ={csch}^{2}⁡(7x-2) \\\\ {coth}^{2}⁡(7x-2) & ={csch}^{2}⁡(7x-2)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{csch}^{3}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)[{csch}^{2}⁡(7x-2)+1]}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2){coth}^{2}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n{\\coth }^{2}⁡\\theta -1={\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =7x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\coth }^{2}⁡(7x-2)-1 & ={\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡(7x-2) \\\\ {\\coth }^{2}⁡(7x-2) & ={\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡(7x-2)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{3}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)[{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡(7x-2)+1]}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2){\\coth }^{2}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186537986 :question/problem "The inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." "The inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186537986 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ 2x-1 & ={log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & ={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y} \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & =y \\\\ y & ={2}^{2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{2x-1}.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡y \\\\ 2x-1 & ={\\log }_{2}⁡y \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & ={2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡y} \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & =y \\\\ y & ={2}^{2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{2x-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537990 :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186537990 :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky} & =x-2 \\\\ {3}^{ky} & =\\frac{5x-10}{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{ky}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ ky & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$" "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky} & =x-2 \\\\ {3}^{ky} & =\\frac{5x-10}{2} \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{ky}) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ ky & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186537991 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x{coth}^{2}⁡x+x{csch}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x{\\coth }^{2}⁡x+x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}{{\\coth }^{4}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186537991 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x{coth}^{2}⁡x+x{csch}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x({coth}^{2}⁡x+{csch}^{2}⁡x)}{({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)({coth}^{2}⁡x+{csch}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x}{({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{\\csc }^{2}⁡x-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n{\\coth }^{2}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x{\\coth }^{2}⁡x+x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}{{\\coth }^{4}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x({\\coth }^{2}⁡x+{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)}{({\\coth }^{2}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)({\\coth }^{2}⁡x+{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x}{({\\coth }^{2}⁡x-{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186538972 :question/worked-solution "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =1-\\frac{{(1/3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(1/3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(1/3)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $cos\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right).$" "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =1-\\frac{{(1/3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(1/3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(1/3)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186538983 :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$" "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186538984 :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35)={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1).$" "Solve the equation ${\\log }_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35)={\\log }_{12}⁡(-12v-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186538984 :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & ={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {v}^{2}+35 & =-12v-1 \\\\ {v}^{2}+12v+36 & =0 \\\\ (v+6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡((-6)^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12(-6)-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡(71) & ={log}_{12}⁡(71).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-6$ is a valid solution." "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & ={\\log }_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {v}^{2}+35 & =-12v-1 \\\\ {v}^{2}+12v+36 & =0 \\\\ (v+6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {\\log }_{12}⁡((-6)^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{12}⁡(-12(-6)-1) \\\\ {\\log }_{12}⁡(71) & ={\\log }_{12}⁡(71).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-6$ is a valid solution."] [:db/cas 17592186538985 :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{3}⁡(x+4)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).$" "Solve the equation ${\\log }_{3}⁡(x+4)=\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(25).$"] [:db/cas 17592186538985 :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡({25}^{1/2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x+4 & =5 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(1+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution." "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+4) & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(25) \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡({25}^{1/2}) \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x+4 & =5 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(1+4) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(25).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution."] [:db/cas 17592186539000 :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x)={log}_{8}⁡(10x-8).$" "Solve the equation ${\\log }_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x)={\\log }_{8}⁡(10x-8).$"] [:db/cas 17592186539000 :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & ={log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+19x & =10x-8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x+8 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+8) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡((-1)^{2}+19(-1)) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10(-1)-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(-18) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(-18)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${log}_{8}⁡(-18)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡((-8)^{2}+19(-8)) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(10(-8)-8) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(-88) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{8}⁡(-88)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${log}_{8}⁡(-88)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-8$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"No solutions.\"" "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & ={\\log }_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+19x & =10x-8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x+8 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+8) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {\\log }_{8}⁡((-1)^{2}+19(-1)) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{8}⁡(10(-1)-8) \\\\ {\\log }_{8}⁡(-18) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{8}⁡(-18)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${\\log }_{8}⁡(-18)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {\\log }_{8}⁡((-8)^{2}+19(-8)) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{8}⁡(10(-8)-8) \\\\ {\\log }_{8}⁡(-88) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{8}⁡(-88)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because ${\\log }_{8}⁡(-88)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-8$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"No solutions.\""] [:db/cas 17592186539022 :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7)={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8).$" "Solve the equation ${\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7)={\\log }_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8).$"] [:db/cas 17592186539022 :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7) & ={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+7 & =2{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}=-1$ has no real solutions. Therefore, our original logarithmic equation has no solutions either." "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+7 & =2{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}=-1$ has no real solutions. Therefore, our original logarithmic equation has no solutions either."] [:db/cas 17592186539161 :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}) \\\\  & =t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}) \\\\  & =t({\\cos }^{2}⁡t-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186539172 :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2})\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =5\\left({\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2})\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t({\\cos }^{2}⁡t-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =5\\left({\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186539480 :answer/value "2+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3})" "2+{\\log }_{4}⁡({x}^{3})"] [:db/cas 17592186539482 :answer/value "6{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{2}⁡y" "6{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+{\\log }_{2}⁡y"] [:db/cas 17592186539507 :answer/value "5{log}_{6}⁡20" "5{\\log }_{6}⁡20"] [:db/cas 17592186539520 :answer/value "{log}_{125}⁡48" "{\\log }_{125}⁡48"] [:db/cas 17592186539534 :answer/value "4+{log}_{4}⁡(2{x}^{3})" "4+{\\log }_{4}⁡(2{x}^{3})"] [:db/cas 17592186539559 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(6{x}^{2})+{log}_{2}⁡y" "{\\log }_{2}⁡(6{x}^{2})+{\\log }_{2}⁡y"] [:db/cas 17592186539576 :answer/value "{log}_{5}⁡10" "{\\log }_{5}⁡10"] [:db/cas 17592186539606 :answer/value "10{log}_{3}⁡20" "10{\\log }_{3}⁡20"] [:db/cas 17592186539607 :answer/value "{log}_{15}⁡48" "{\\log }_{15}⁡48"] [:d