 rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms and the distributive law, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}y) & =6({log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{2}⁡y) \\\\  & =6{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+6{log}_{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms and the distributive law, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}y) & =6({\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+{\\log }_{2}⁡y) \\\\  & =6{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+6{\\log }_{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540043 :question/problem "If ${log}_{5}⁡s=4$ and ${log}_{5}⁡t=5,$ then ${log}_{5}⁡(st)=${{field-1}}." "If ${\\log }_{5}⁡s=4$ and ${\\log }_{5}⁡t=5,$ then ${\\log }_{5}⁡(st)=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186540043 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(st) & ={log}_{5}⁡s+{log}_{5}⁡t \\\\  & =4+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡(st) & ={\\log }_{5}⁡s+{\\log }_{5}⁡t \\\\  & =4+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540044 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =8(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3=8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =8(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3=8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540045 :question/problem "Express ${log}_{6}⁡8+{log}_{6}⁡3+{log}_{6}⁡2$ as a single logarithm." "Express ${\\log }_{6}⁡8+{\\log }_{6}⁡3+{\\log }_{6}⁡2$ as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186540045 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡8+{log}_{6}⁡3+{log}_{6}⁡2 & ={log}_{6}⁡(8\\cdot 3)+{log}_{6}⁡2 \\\\  & ={log}_{6}⁡24+{log}_{6}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms once more, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡24+{log}_{6}⁡2 & ={log}_{6}⁡(24\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ={log}_{6}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{6}⁡8+{\\log }_{6}⁡3+{\\log }_{6}⁡2 & ={\\log }_{6}⁡(8\\cdot 3)+{\\log }_{6}⁡2 \\\\  & ={\\log }_{6}⁡24+{\\log }_{6}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms once more, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{6}⁡24+{\\log }_{6}⁡2 & ={\\log }_{6}⁡(24\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{6}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540050 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =10+2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =10+2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540052 :question/problem "Express $5{log}_{3}⁡4+5{log}_{3}⁡5$ as a single logarithm." "Express $5{\\log }_{3}⁡4+5{\\log }_{3}⁡5$ as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186540052 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{log}_{3}⁡4+5{log}_{3}⁡5 & =5({log}_{3}⁡4+{log}_{3}⁡5) \\\\  & =5{log}_{3}⁡(4\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =5{log}_{3}⁡20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5{log}_{3}⁡4+5{log}_{3}⁡5=5{log}_{3}⁡20.\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\log }_{3}⁡4+5{\\log }_{3}⁡5 & =5({\\log }_{3}⁡4+{\\log }_{3}⁡5) \\\\  & =5{\\log }_{3}⁡(4\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =5{\\log }_{3}⁡20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5{\\log }_{3}⁡4+5{\\log }_{3}⁡5=5{\\log }_{3}⁡20.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540054 :question/problem "${log}_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2})=$" "${\\log }_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186540054 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2})={log}_{2}⁡32+{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince $32={2}^{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡32={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2}) & ={log}_{2}⁡32+{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5+{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2})={\\log }_{2}⁡32+{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince $32={2}^{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{2}⁡32={\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{5})=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2}) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡32+{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5+{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540062 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $cot$ is the reciprocal of $tan.$ So first we need to compute tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{PR}{QR}=\\frac{3}{QR}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{Q}^{2}-P{R}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{5})^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-1}=2.\n$$" "Remember that $\\cot $ is the reciprocal of $tan.$ So first we need to compute tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{PR}{QR}=\\frac{3}{QR}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{Q}^{2}-P{R}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{5})^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-1}=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540074 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $csc$ is the reciprocal of $sin.$ So first we need to compute sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{QR}{PR}=\\frac{QR}{15}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{R}^{2}-P{Q}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}-{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225-81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{QR}{15}=\\frac{12}{15}=\\frac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡P={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" "Remember that $\\csc $ is the reciprocal of $sin.$ So first we need to compute sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{QR}{PR}=\\frac{QR}{15}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{R}^{2}-P{Q}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}-{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225-81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{QR}{15}=\\frac{12}{15}=\\frac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡P={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540076 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8 & =4(\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+\\operatorname{log}⁡8) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\cdot 4\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8=4\\operatorname{log}⁡64.\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8 & =4(\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+\\operatorname{log}⁡8) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\cdot 4\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8=4\\operatorname{log}⁡64.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540078 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4-3{sech}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4-3{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540078 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\theta )={sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\int {sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4-3{sech}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}\\theta +\\int (-3{sech}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\int \\,\\text{d}\\theta -3\\int {sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta -3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\theta )={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4-3{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}\\theta +\\int (-3{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\int \\,\\text{d}\\theta -3\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta -3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540092 :question/problem "${log}_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3})=$" "${\\log }_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186540092 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3})={log}_{4}⁡16+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16={4}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡16={log}_{4}⁡({4}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3}) & ={log}_{4}⁡16+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3})={\\log }_{4}⁡16+{\\log }_{4}⁡({x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16={4}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{4}⁡16={\\log }_{4}⁡({4}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3}) & ={\\log }_{4}⁡16+{\\log }_{4}⁡({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2+{\\log }_{4}⁡({x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540094 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq) & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq)) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p\\cdot q\\cdot pq) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡({p}^{2}{q}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq) & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq)) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p\\cdot q\\cdot pq) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡({p}^{2}{q}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540109 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{log}⁡t+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3}) & =4(\\operatorname{log}⁡t+\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡(t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{1+2+3}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{log}⁡t+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3}) & =4(\\operatorname{log}⁡t+\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡(t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{1+2+3}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540110 :question/problem "Consider two sets of $39$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{39},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{39}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=663,\\,{S}_{yy}=1\\,092,\\,{S}_{xy}=975.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." "Consider two sets of $39$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{39},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{39}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=663,\\,{S}_{yy}=1\\,092,\\,{S}_{xy}=975.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186540110 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=975.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{975}{39} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=975.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov} (x,y) & =\\frac{975}{39} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540112 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2})=\\operatorname{log}⁡100+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100={10}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡100=\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡100+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2})=\\operatorname{log}⁡100+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100={10}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡100=\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡100+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186540113 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms and the distributive law, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{log}⁡(6z) & =9(\\operatorname{log}⁡6+\\operatorname{log}⁡z) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{log}⁡6+9\\operatorname{log}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡a+{\\log }_{n}⁡b={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms and the distributive law, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{log}⁡(6z) & =9(\\operatorname{log}⁡6+\\operatorname{log}⁡z) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{log}⁡6+9\\operatorname{log}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541060 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1.\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541076 :question/worked-solution "![](images/e0/e087efb9eb18158005722dd6628cc8f19b22a2b33cad8f67205b8ea398e62c54.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" "![](images/e0/e087efb9eb18158005722dd6628cc8f19b22a2b33cad8f67205b8ea398e62c54.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541091 :question/worked-solution "![](images/57/5778175613309c2b4b8007291ad122b879de1cd409ad3513f3f786c8f4885927.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/57/5778175613309c2b4b8007291ad122b879de1cd409ad3513f3f786c8f4885927.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541118 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{50} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{63} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}=1.\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{50} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{63} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541137 :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f0c1c9df42fc2ed772353baec0f81e796485d8bf3c1cd9dde234e74e39a04c56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/f0/f0c1c9df42fc2ed772353baec0f81e796485d8bf3c1cd9dde234e74e39a04c56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541147 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{7} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1.\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{7} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186541154 :question/worked-solution "![](images/05/0534847b2363ff75e053b9d05736ff367f2ad6da527b7100de0018d003c4c9df.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" "![](images/05/0534847b2363ff75e053b9d05736ff367f2ad6da527b7100de0018d003c4c9df.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186542116 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that, by multiplying by $5$ on both sides,  the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{3}^{x}=15\\cdot {4}^{x}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{3}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x})={log}_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x})\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use  the product law and the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(15)+{log}_{3}⁡({4}^{x}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(15)+x{log}_{3}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(15)+x{log}_{3}⁡4 \\\\ x-x{log}_{3}⁡4 & ={log}_{3}⁡(15) \\\\ x(1-{log}_{3}⁡4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(15) \\\\ x & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(15)}{1-{log}_{3}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(15)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡15}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}≈2.464\\,974, \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}≈1.261\\,860.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2.464\\,974}{1-1.261\\,860}≈-9.413,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "First, notice that, by multiplying by $5$ on both sides,  the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{3}^{x}=15\\cdot {4}^{x}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{3}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n{\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{x})={\\log }_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x})\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that ${\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use  the product law and the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x}) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(15)+{\\log }_{3}⁡({4}^{x}) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(15)+x{\\log }_{3}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(15)+x{\\log }_{3}⁡4 \\\\ x-x{\\log }_{3}⁡4 & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(15) \\\\ x(1-{\\log }_{3}⁡4) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(15) \\\\ x & =\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡(15)}{1-{\\log }_{3}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡(15)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡15}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}≈2.464\\,974, \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}≈1.261\\,860.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2.464\\,974}{1-1.261\\,860}≈-9.413,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186542131 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{log}_{3}⁡(x-1)-5<7.$" "Solve the inequality $3{\\log }_{3}⁡(x-1)-5<7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186542131 :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{3}⁡(x-1)-5 & <7 \\\\ 3{log}_{3}⁡(x-1) & <12 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x-1) & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $3.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x-1)} & <{3}^{4} \\\\ x-1 & <81 \\\\ x & <82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-1>0\\,⇒\\,x>1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<82,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,82).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>1,$ which can be expressed as $(1,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,82)\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,82)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (1,82),$ which can also be written as $1<x<82.$" "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\log }_{3}⁡(x-1)-5 & <7 \\\\ 3{\\log }_{3}⁡(x-1) & <12 \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(x-1) & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $3.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡(x-1)} & <{3}^{4} \\\\ x-1 & <81 \\\\ x & <82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-1>0\\,⇒\\,x>1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<82,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,82).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>1,$ which can be expressed as $(1,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,82)\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,82)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (1,82),$ which can also be written as $1<x<82.$"] [:db/cas 17592186542149 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x-2} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x-2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx-2=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2=x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x-2} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x-2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx-2=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2=x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186542160 :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{5}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+4} & ={2}^{2-x} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{x+4}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4=(2-x){log}_{5}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =(2-x){log}_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+4 & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-x{log}_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+x{log}_{5}⁡(2) & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x(1+{log}_{5}⁡(2)) & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4}{1+{log}_{5}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡(2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}≈0.430\\,677.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2\\cdot 0.430\\,677-4}{1+0.430\\,677}≈-2.194,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "First, we take ${log}_{5}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+4} & ={2}^{2-x} \\\\ {\\log }_{5}⁡({5}^{x+4}) & ={\\log }_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4={\\log }_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4=(2-x){\\log }_{5}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =(2-x){\\log }_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+4 & =2{\\log }_{5}⁡(2)-x{\\log }_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+x{\\log }_{5}⁡(2) & =2{\\log }_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x(1+{\\log }_{5}⁡(2)) & =2{\\log }_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2{\\log }_{5}⁡(2)-4}{1+{\\log }_{5}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{5}⁡(2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}≈0.430\\,677.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2\\cdot 0.430\\,677-4}{1+0.430\\,677}≈-2.194,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186542173 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)-2>0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "Solve the inequality $5{\\log }_{2}⁡(x+4)-2>0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186542173 :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)-2 & >0 \\\\ 5{log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & >2 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & >\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)} & >{2}^{2/5} \\\\ x+4 & >\\sqrt[5]{4} \\\\ x & >\\sqrt[5]{4}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+4>0\\,⇒\\,x>-4\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,$ which can be expressed as $(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-4,$ which can be expressed as $(-4,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty ),\\text{ which can also be written as }x>\\sqrt[5]{4}-4.\n$$" "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\log }_{2}⁡(x+4)-2 & >0 \\\\ 5{\\log }_{2}⁡(x+4) & >2 \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(x+4) & >\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡(x+4)} & >{2}^{2/5} \\\\ x+4 & >\\sqrt[5]{4} \\\\ x & >\\sqrt[5]{4}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+4>0\\,⇒\\,x>-4\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,$ which can be expressed as $(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-4,$ which can be expressed as $(-4,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty ),\\text{ which can also be written as }x>\\sqrt[5]{4}-4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186542191 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186542197 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =(x+2)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =(x+2)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186542206 :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}\\,x+\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2bb2678804e79eb60cf9e1b16df60b8dad4ec4ccf8b2a9234a838c394ab9f3ac.png)" "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}\\,x+\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2bb2678804e79eb60cf9e1b16df60b8dad4ec4ccf8b2a9234a838c394ab9f3ac.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186542215 :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =7{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/4d/4d58fd3550e70915f3bc2a5e8ecd64cc4ef6c154a84ca536a5ca6ef6ddf49450.png)" "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =7{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/4d/4d58fd3550e70915f3bc2a5e8ecd64cc4ef6c154a84ca536a5ca6ef6ddf49450.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186542227 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186542646 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3}+x)" "{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{3}+x)"] [:db/cas 17592186542667 :answer/value "2{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+{log}_{2}⁡(z)" "2{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)+{\\log }_{2}⁡(z)"] [:db/cas 17592186542671 :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y}\\right)" "{\\log }_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186542678 :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" "2{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(z)"] [:db/cas 17592186542699 :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-9" "{\\log }_{3}⁡(5)+{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)-9"] [:db/cas 17592186542701 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{5}\\right)" "{\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\fr