{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z)" "2{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)-{\\log }_{2}⁡(z)"] [:db/cas 17592186542945 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(2)" "{\\log }_{2}⁡(2)"] [:db/cas 17592186542983 :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x)}{{log}_{3}⁡(y)}" "\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)}{{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)}"] [:db/cas 17592186542994 :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" "2{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)+{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(z)"] [:db/cas 17592186542999 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3}-x)" "{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{3}-x)"] [:db/cas 17592186543012 :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(5)-{log}_{3}⁡(x)-9" "{\\log }_{3}⁡(5)-{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)-9"] [:db/cas 17592186543022 :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{log}_{3}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" "{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)+{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(z)"] [:db/cas 17592186543096 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3})" "{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{3})"] [:db/cas 17592186543104 :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{log}_{3}⁡(y)" "{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)+{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)"] [:db/cas 17592186543117 :answer/value "{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{5}}\\right)" "{\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{5}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186543125 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(35)" "{\\log }_{2}⁡(35)"] [:db/cas 17592186543160 :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x}y)" "{\\log }_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x}y)"] [:db/cas 17592186543175 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(t+7)" "{\\log }_{2}⁡(t+7)"] [:db/cas 17592186543193 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})" "{\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{2})"] [:db/cas 17592186543236 :answer/value "2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z)" "2{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)-2{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)-{\\log }_{2}⁡(z)"] [:db/cas 17592186543239 :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡(y)" "{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)-{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)"] [:db/cas 17592186543240 :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}\\right)" "{\\log }_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186543275 :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡({t}^{7})" "{\\log }_{2}⁡({t}^{7})"] [:db/cas 17592186543281 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)-2{log}_{2}⁡(y)+{log}_{2}⁡(z)" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)-2{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)+{\\log }_{2}⁡(z)"] [:db/cas 17592186543472 :question/problem "Find the value of ${log}_{z}⁡(512)$ if ${log}_{z}⁡(8)=0.3.$" "Find the value of ${\\log }_{z}⁡(512)$ if ${\\log }_{z}⁡(8)=0.3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186543472 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{z}⁡(512) & ={log}_{z}⁡({8}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {log}_{z}⁡(8) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{z}⁡(512) & ={\\log }_{z}⁡({8}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\log }_{z}⁡(8) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543479 :question/problem "Express ${log}_{2}⁡(7)-{log}_{2}⁡(5)$ as a single logarithm." "Express ${\\log }_{2}⁡(7)-{\\log }_{2}⁡(5)$ as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186543479 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(7)-{log}_{2}⁡(5)={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{5}\\right)\n$$" "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{2}⁡(7)-{\\log }_{2}⁡(5)={\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{5}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543483 :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=$" "${\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186543483 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right) & ={log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(x)-{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543487 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543491 :question/problem "${log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)=$" "${\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186543491 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)={log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z)={log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z) & ={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{1/2})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)={\\log }_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{\\log }_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{\\log }_{2}⁡(z)={\\log }_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{\\log }_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{\\log }_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{\\log }_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{\\log }_{2}⁡(z) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡({x}^{1/2})+{\\log }_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{\\log }_{2}⁡(z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+2{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)-{\\log }_{2}⁡(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+2{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)-{\\log }_{2}⁡(z).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543498 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543502 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{ex}{5}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ex)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{ex}{5}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ex)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543519 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient quotient and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{6})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient quotient and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{6})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543524 :question/problem "If ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, then ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=$" "If ${\\log }_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${\\log }_{5}⁡(y)=2$, then ${\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186543524 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)=2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2=12.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=12.\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & ={\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{2})+{\\log }_{5}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{\\log }_{5}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{5}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${\\log }_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${\\log }_{5}⁡(y)=2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2{\\log }_{5}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{5}⁡(y)=2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2=12.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543525 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543526 :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{27}\\right)=$" "${\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{27}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186543526 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{27}\\right) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5x)-{log}_{3}⁡(27) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{27}\\right) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(5x)-{\\log }_{3}⁡(27) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(5)+{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)-{\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{3}) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(5)+{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543531 :question/problem "If ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4$, then ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=$" "If ${\\log }_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${\\log }_{2}⁡(y)=4$, then ${\\log }_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186543531 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y) & =k+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=k+12.\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${\\log }_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3}) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+{\\log }_{2}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{2}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${\\log }_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${\\log }_{2}⁡(y)=4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{2}⁡(y) & =k+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=k+12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543537 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3/4})\n$$\n\nHence $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$" "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3/4})\n$$\n\nHence $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186543541 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-5{log}_{5}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." "Express $\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{5}⁡(x)-5{\\log }_{5}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186543541 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-5{log}_{5}⁡(y) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{1/2})-{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\sqrt{x})-{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{5}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{5}⁡(x)-5{\\log }_{5}⁡(y) & ={\\log }_{5}⁡({x}^{1/2})-{\\log }_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{5}⁡(\\sqrt{x})-{\\log }_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{5}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543542 :question/problem "![](images/25/2593d513ce130090a84cc26e71bf321960712a6ccb2d8ff82e55e4728cc0657c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ The curves intersect at the points $A,B,C,$ and $D,$ as shown. Determine the exact values of the $x$-coordinates of points $A$ and $D,$ in radians.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n{{field-1}}\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/25/2593d513ce130090a84cc26e71bf321960712a6ccb2d8ff82e55e4728cc0657c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x$ and the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ The curves intersect at the points $A,B,C,$ and $D,$ as shown. Determine the exact values of the $x$-coordinates of points $A$ and $D,$ in radians.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n{{field-1}}\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186543542 :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives the equation $2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining the solutions and ordering them from smallest to largest, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n$\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n$\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives the equation $2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining the solutions and ordering them from smallest to largest, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n$\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n$\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186543547 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =k-m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =k-m.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543549 :question/problem "$7{log}_{2}⁡t=$" "$7{\\log }_{2}⁡t=$"] [:db/cas 17592186543549 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({c}^{m})=m\\operatorname{log}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n7{log}_{2}⁡t={log}_{2}⁡({t}^{7}).\n$$" "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({c}^{m})=m\\operatorname{log}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n7{\\log }_{2}⁡t={\\log }_{2}⁡({t}^{7}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543555 :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $cos\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}, $\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $\\cos \\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}, $\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186543573 :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)=$" "${\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186543573 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z}).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)={\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})={\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{\\log }_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z}).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{\\log }_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})=2{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)=2{\\log }_{3}⁡(x)+3{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(z).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543577 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." "Express $\\frac{1}{3}{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)-{\\log }_{4}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186543577 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y) & ={log}_{4}⁡({x}^{1/3})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)+{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡(a)-{\\log }_{n}⁡(b)={\\log }_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${\\log }_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{\\log }_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)-{\\log }_{4}⁡(y) & ={\\log }_{4}⁡({x}^{1/3})-{\\log }_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x})-{\\log }_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={\\log }_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543721 :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/07/074eb42aff2c78d93136fc9e56b2420450f72c5d34c6b12da8bf84e080db75ea.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/ce/cea2b2ddd6b211073b579d71bd8ed08405fa04e354825a124acb71bedf413295.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/bb/bbe07b71b3c82cde2e9175b06904905206984b04ca9a8ccd746b051212f5e8d8.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/07/074eb42aff2c78d93136fc9e56b2420450f72c5d34c6b12da8bf84e080db75ea.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/ce/cea2b2ddd6b211073b579d71bd8ed08405fa04e354825a124acb71bedf413295.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/bb/bbe07b71b3c82cde2e9175b06904905206984b04ca9a8ccd746b051212f5e8d8.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543943 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle 8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right).\n$$" "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle 8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543943 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)=8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi({sin}^{2}⁡\\phi+{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi({sin}^{2}⁡\\phi+{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) & =8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot\\left(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\phi\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)=8\\cdot\\left(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\phi\\right)\n$$" "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)=8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\phi+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\phi)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\phi+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\phi)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) & =8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot\\left(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\phi\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)=8\\cdot\\left(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\phi\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186543947 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle 8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operato