 }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186544813 :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" "The given polar curve is the 