lify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx={log}_{3}⁡(5)\n$$" "We take ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & =5 \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx={\\log }_{3}⁡(5)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544817 :question/problem "![](images/66/66616f493d84804ec9c7c2a1e3b8598404f1f57ffda2e0a68718342f508010af.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" "![](images/66/66616f493d84804ec9c7c2a1e3b8598404f1f57ffda2e0a68718342f508010af.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186544817 :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $7$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=0,$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0 \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the fifth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fourth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{9} & ={\\theta }_{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{10} & ={\\theta }_{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the sixth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fifth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{11} & ={\\theta }_{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{12} & ={\\theta }_{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the seventh petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the sixth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{13} & ={\\theta }_{11}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{14} & ={\\theta }_{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $7$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{7},0]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\u