 {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the fifth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fourth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{9} & ={\\theta }_{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{10} & ={\\theta }_{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the sixth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fifth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{11} & ={\\theta }_{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{12} & ={\\theta }_{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the seventh petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the sixth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{13} & ={\\theta }_{11}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{14} & ={\\theta }_{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $7$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{7},0]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{7},\\frac{2\\pi }{7}]}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{2\\pi }{7},\\frac{3\\pi }{7}]}_{4\\text{th}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{3\\pi }{7},\\frac{4\\pi }{7}]}_{5\\text{th}}\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{4\\pi }{7},\\frac{5\\pi }{7}]}_{6\\text{th}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{5\\pi }{7},\\frac{6\\pi }{7}]}_{7\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544821 :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that the slope of the tangent line at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\nm=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\ny-{y}_{0}=m(x-{x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ are the coordinates of the point on the curve that correspond to $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Let's work these out. \n\nIn part 1, we found that\n\n$\\,$ $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,y(\\theta )=6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{0} & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2}).\n$$ \n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{0} & =m(x-{x}_{0}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b8546c0b6c51577acf25587fc1ebfc88e15e5f3b9456a64c499d640851bb44d0.png)" "Previously, we found that the slope of the tangent line at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\nm=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\ny-{y}_{0}=m(x-{x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ are the coordinates of the point on the curve that correspond to $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Let's work these out. \n\nIn part 1, we found that\n\n$\\,$ $x(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,y(\\theta )=6{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{0} & =6{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{9}{2}).\n$$ \n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{0} & =m(x-{x}_{0}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{9}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b8546c0b6c51577acf25587fc1ebfc88e15e5f3b9456a64c499d640851bb44d0.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186544825 :question/problem "![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" "![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186544825 :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/42/428af134250df6268b97998db880e33f88a6b5bd32859036e29e04fc1861e0c9.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544828 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544832 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $9\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n9\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}].$  Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $9\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n9\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}].$  Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544834 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544836 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3}-13 & =25 \\\\ {5}^{2a+3} & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{5}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3} & =38 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2a+3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(38) \\\\ 2a+3 & ={log}_{5}⁡(38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ 2a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\na≈-0.370\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3}-13 & =25 \\\\ {5}^{2a+3} & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{5}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a+3} & =38 \\\\ {\\log }_{5}⁡({5}^{2a+3}) & ={\\log }_{5}⁡(38) \\\\ 2a+3 & ={\\log }_{5}⁡(38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ 2a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\na≈-0.370\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186544837 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544838 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/caacb78b0f37dcc58d6568a571c99bc569c7eb95aeacf724d5df33e35ba21fa7.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/caacb78b0f37dcc58d6568a571c99bc569c7eb95aeacf724d5df33e35ba21fa7.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544839 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $7\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $7\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}].\n$$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{\\pi /14}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544843 :question/problem "![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" "![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186544843 :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ]}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =0$ to $\\theta =\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ]}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =0$ to $\\theta =\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544844 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/c9/c94a6f3da0e2feb5421ad8652da0dde170d914ea4404a70c37a4112293fe810e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)≈1.18,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/c9/c94a6f3da0e2feb5421ad8652da0dde170d914ea4404a70c37a4112293fe810e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\arctan \\,\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)≈1.18,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186544845 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\n![](images/7f/7f1a9ba1b4d9a86e23b8634dedaf6d4e1a651efbe4fc7aa27ac4dbb5d443018f.png)\n\nSince there are $12$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =12\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =12\\cdot 8{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =48{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12} \\\\  & =48[(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =48(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\n![](images/7f/7f1a9ba1b4d9a86e23b8634dedaf6d4e1a651efbe4fc7aa27ac4dbb5d443018f.png)\n\nSince there are $12$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =12\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =12\\cdot 8{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =48{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12} \\\\  & =48[(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =48(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544846 :question/worked-solution "Taking ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-2x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4-2x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ 4-2x & ={log}_{3}⁡({2}^{1/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-2x=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ -2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-4 \\\\ x & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈1.842\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places" "Taking ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-2x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{4-2x}) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ 4-2x & ={\\log }_{3}⁡({2}^{1/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-2x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{3}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ -2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-4 \\\\ x & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈1.842\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places"] [:db/cas 17592186544848 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\n![](images/e1/e1f3651f2c120b65063015a17a7e657ccb5b2e87df475660785d35748c05557a.png)\n\nSince there are $8$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =8\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =32[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =32(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\n![](images/e1/e1f3651f2c120b65063015a17a7e657ccb5b2e87df475660785d35748c05557a.png)\n\nSince there are $8$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =8\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =32[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =32(\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544849 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544858 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPreviously, we've found that\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right) & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPreviously, we've found that\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right) & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{\\sec }^{2}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{\\sec }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sec }^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544862 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544868 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c35b0e3c4e51b164097e6deb11e4418726927f478f934e41d1250388e9a27e5.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{(\\theta -\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =20[(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c35b0e3c4e51b164097e6deb11e4418726927f478f934e41d1250388e9a27e5.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{16}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{(\\theta -\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =20[(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186544870 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1d/1d131288e3d38767e1d82b68777e69667711e98665101093f3b7e544e9caea1e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)=0.404\\,892…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi +0.404\\,892…≈3.55,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1d/1d131288e3d38767e1d82b68777e69667711e98665101093f3b7e544e9caea1e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\arctan \\,\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)=0.404\\,892…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi +0.404\\,892…≈3.55,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186544881 :question/worked-solution "Taking ${log}_{11}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{4-y} & =\\sqrt[4]{3} \\\\ {log}_{11}⁡({11}^{4-y}) & ={log}_{11}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{3}) \\\\ 4-y & ={log}_{11}⁡({3}^{1/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-y=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{11}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} \\\\ -y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}-4 \\\\ y & =4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈3.885\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Taking ${log}_{11}$ of both sides of the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{4-y} & =\\sqrt[4]{3} \\\\ {\\log }_{11}⁡({11}^{4-y}) & ={\\log }_{11}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{3}) \\\\ 4-y & ={\\log }_{11}⁡({3}^{1/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n4-y=\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{11}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and then solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} \\\\ -y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}-4 \\\\ y & =4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{4\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈3.885\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186544896 :question/problem "![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" "![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186544896 :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186545224 :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡3" "{\\ln }^{2}⁡3"] [:db/cas 17592186546273 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}(3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}(3{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186546289 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(8{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(8{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186546294 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}({sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )-2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}({\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )-2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] [:db/cas 17592186546295 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}\\right))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " "\\fr