drant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{5}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{39}+\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}}{39} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}-12\\sqrt{2}}{39}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=12,$ and $a+b=17.$" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡u+{\\cos }^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡u=1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡u & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{5}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{5}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{39}+\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}}{39} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}-12\\sqrt{2}}{39}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=12,$ and $a+b=17.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552429 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{8}+\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-1\\sqrt{14}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,b=1,$ and $a+b=4.$" "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡v+{\\cos }^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{\\sin }^{2}⁡v=1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡v & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{8}+\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-1\\sqrt{14}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,b=1,$ and $a+b=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552437 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-5)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-5)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-5s}\\,L\\{g(t+5)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+5) & =cos\\,(5\\pi (t+5)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t+25\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡25\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡25\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+5)\\} & =L\\{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =-L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-5)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-5s}\\,L\\{g(t+5)\\} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-5)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-5)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-5s}\\,L\\{g(t+5)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+5) & =\\cos \\,(5\\pi (t+5)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t+25\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡25\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡25\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+5)\\} & =L\\{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =-L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-5)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-5s}\\,L\\{g(t+5)\\} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552438 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{20}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}+2\\sqrt{5}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=2,$ and $a+b=7.$" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡v+{\\cos }^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡v=1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡v & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{20}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}+2\\sqrt{5}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=2,$ and $a+b=7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552445 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-3)sin\\,(2(t-3))\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-3)\\sin \\,(2(t-3))\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [:db/cas 17592186552445 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3)sin\\,(2(t-3)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)=sin\\,(2(t-3)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-3)sin\\,(2(t-3))\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3)\\sin \\,(2(t-3)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)=\\sin \\,(2(t-3)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-3)\\sin \\,(2(t-3))\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552460 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{14}-\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{14} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}+5}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=6,b=5,$ and $a+b=11.$" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡v+{\\cos }^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{\\sin }^{2}⁡v=1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡v & =1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{14}-\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{14} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}+5}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=6,b=5,$ and $a+b=11.$"] [:db/cas 17592186552478 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{2}^{2}(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{15}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+8\\sqrt{2}}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡v+{\\cos }^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡v=1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡v & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{2}^{2}(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{15}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+8\\sqrt{2}}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552481 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{26}-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{26} \\\\  & =-\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡u+{\\cos }^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡u=1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡u & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{26}-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{26} \\\\  & =-\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552504 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+16t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0+32\\pi -1)-(0-1) \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+16t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0+32\\pi -1)-(0-1) \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552510 :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}((x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}((x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552515 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+6) & =sin\\,(7\\pi (t+6)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t+42\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡42\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡42\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}\n$$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+6)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+6) & =\\sin \\,(7\\pi (t+6)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t+42\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡42\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡42\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}\n$$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+6)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553498 :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+4}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\sin \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+4}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$"] [:db/cas 17592186553498 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-2)=sY(s)+2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $f(t)=sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{sin\\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s+4}+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\sin \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\sin \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-2)=sY(s)+2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\sin \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $f(t)=\\sin \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\sin \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{\\sin \\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\sin \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s+4}+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553505 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /8}3{csc}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /8}3{\\csc }^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186553505 :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /12$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /8}3{csc}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}3{csc}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u))\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(0+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we let $u=4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /12$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /8}3{\\csc }^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}3{\\csc }^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\csc }^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u))\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(0+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186553507 :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=3y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 3y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot {[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡x}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=3y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 3y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot {[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{\\sin }^{4}⁡x}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]]
